\v 22 As long as the¦3433 earth¦3434 \add continues to\add* exist,
\q1 seedtime and¦3436 harvest¦3436 time¦3433,
\q1 cold¦3437 and heat¦3438,
\q1 summer¦3439 and winter¦3440,
\q1 day¦3441 and night¦3442,
\q1 will¦3444 never \add stop happening\add*.
\c 9
\s1 God's agreement with Noah
\rem /s1 God's Covenant With Noah; The Covenant with Noah
\p
\v 1 Then¦3446 God¦3447 blessed¦3446 Noah¦3450 and his¦3453 sons¦3453 and said¦3454 to¦3455 them¦3455, “Be¦3456 fruitful¦3456 and multiply¦3457 and fill¦3458 the¦3461 earth¦3461.\x + \xo 9:1: \xt Gen 1:28.\x*
\v 2 \add Now\add* every living creature on the earth¦3470 will¦3485 be¦3465 afraid and terrified of¦3472 you, including¦3464 every bird¦3474 \add that flies\add* in the sky, along with¦3476 every creature that moves on land¦3470, and all the fish¦3482 in the ocean. I have put them \add all\add* under your¦3463 authority.
\v 3 \add From now on\add* every creature that¦3490 is¦3493 alive¦3493 and moves can be eaten as food—just like¦3497 I \add previously\add* provided you green¦3497 plants¦3498 for food, now I provide you everything¦3503 \add for food\add*—
\v 4 the only exception is meat¦3507 with¦3508 it's life¦3508 blood¦3509 \add still\add* in¦3508 it.\x + \xo 9:4: \xt Lev 7:26-27; 17:10-14; 19:26; Deu 12:16,23; 15:23.\x*
\v 5 Also¦3513, I will¦3518 certainly require blood¦3516 as payment for people's lives¦3517: if anyone murders another person, I require that the¦3519 murderer must die, and¦3513 even if an animal¦3522 kills a human¦3525 being, I require that it¦3523 must die.
\v 6 \add Yes,\add* if¦3541 anyone¦3535 sheds¦3535 human blood, the others must¦3540 shed¦3540 the murderer's blood, because¦3541 God¦3543 made¦3544 human beings in¦3538 his¦3539 image¦3542.\x + \xo 9:6: \xt Exo 20:13; Gen 1:26.\x*
\v 7 \add So then,\add* be¦3550 fruitful¦3550 and multiply—have children on¦3556 the¦3553 earth¦3553 and become numerous.\x + \xo 9:7: \xt Gen 1:28.\x*
\p
\v 8 Then God¦3560 said¦3559 to¦3561 Noah¦3563 and¦3564 his¦3566 sons¦3566,
\v 9 “Now listen, I am¦3572 making an agreement¦3575 with¦3576 you and¦3577 your future descendants¦3579,
\v 10 and \add it's also\add* with every living creature¦3585 that is¦3588 with you: with the birds¦3589, the livestock¦3590, and every living thing on the earth with you, from¦3596 all¦3596 that came¦3597 out¦3597 of¦3597 the \add wooden\add* chest to¦3599 every \add future\add* living thing on the earth.
\v 11 My¦3606 agreement¦3606 that I'm establishing¦3603 with¦3607 you¦3607 is that all¦3611 living things will never die from¦3615 a flood again. \add That's right,\add* I will never \add use\add* a flood again to¦3622 destroy¦3622 everything on the¦3623 earth¦3623.”
\v 12 Then¦3625 God¦3626 added, “This¦3627 is¦3628 how I'll show everyone¦3638 that I've made¦3634 this¦3627 agreement¦3630 with you and¦3625 with all the¦3628 living¦3641 creatures¦3640 that are with you, along with all future generations¦3644:
\v 13 I'll¦3650 place my¦3649 rainbow among the clouds¦3651, and it'll¦3652 be a sign¦3653 of¦3653 the agreement¦3654 between¦3655 me¦3655 and \add all life\add* on¦3651 the earth¦3657.
\v 14 So¦3659 whenever¦3660 I¦3660 cause clouds to form \add in the sky\add* and¦3659 a rainbow is visible in the clouds,
\v 15 then¦3669 I'll¦3670 remember¦3669 my¦3672 agreement¦3672 that's¦3673 between¦3674 me¦3674 and you and every¦3677 kind of¦3677 living creature¦3679, and a flood¦3689 will¦3686 never again¦3687 destroy¦3690 everything¦3677 living.
\v 16 So¦3695 whenever a rainbow appears among the clouds¦3697, I'll¦3698 see¦3698 it¦3698 and remember¦3699 my permanent¦3701 agreement¦3700 with¦3697 every¦3705 living¦3708 creature¦3707 that¦3712 lives \add and will live\add* on¦3697 the earth¦3715.”
\v 17 Then¦3717 God¦3718 said¦3717 to¦3719 Noah¦3721, “\add The rainbow\add* is¦3723 the sign¦3723 of¦3723 the agreement¦3725 that¦3726 I¦3727 have¦3727 established¦3727 between¦3728 me¦3728 and¦3717 every living thing on¦3734 the earth¦3736.”
\s1 Noah's children
\rem /s1 Noah's Sons; The Sons of Noah; Noah and His Sons
\p
\v 18 Noah's¦3742 sons¦3740 who¦3743 came¦3743 out¦3743 of¦3740 the \add wooden\add* chest \add after the flood\add* were¦3739 Yafet¦3749 (Japheth), Shem¦3747, and¦3739 Ham. (Ham became Canaan's¦3753 father¦3752).
\v 19 They were¦3757 Noah's¦3759 three sons¦3757 and¦3760 from¦3760 them the¦3764 whole¦3762 earth¦3764 became populated.
\p
\v 20 \add After a while,\add* Noah¦3767, who was a farmer, planted¦3770 \add some grape\add* vines,
\v 21 and \add in due course\add* he drank¦3773 some¦3774 of¦3774 \add his\add* wine¦3776 and got drunk¦3777, and he \add fell asleep\add* naked in his tent¦3780.
\v 22 Then Ham¦3784 (Canaan's¦3786 father) saw¦3783 his father's¦3789 nakedness¦3788 then went outside¦3794 and¦3790 told¦3790 his brothers¦3793 \add what he'd seen\add*.
\v 23 So¦3796 Shem¦3797 and Yafet¦3798 took¦3796 a robe, and holding it¦3796 between their shoulders¦3805 they walked¦3807 backwards¦3808 into the tent and covered¦3809 their father's¦3812 nakedness. (Their¦3806 faces¦3813 were¦3814 towards the tent entrance, so they didn't¦3817 see¦3818 his nakedness.)
\v 24 \add Eventually,\add* Noah¦3821 woke up from¦3822 his drunken sleep and¦3823 found out what his youngest¦3831 son¦3830 (Ham) had¦3827 done¦3827 to¦3829 him¦3829
\v 25 and said¦3833, “May Canaan¦3835 be¦3834 cursed¦3834 and become a slave¦3836 of¦3836 slaves¦3837 to his¦3839 brothers¦3839!”
\v 26 Then¦3841 he¦3841 added, “Praise Yahweh¦3843 who's Shem's god¦3844. May Canaan¦3847 become Shem's slave¦3848.
\v 27 May God¦3852 expand Yafet's territory and¦3858 may¦3851 he¦3851 live in¦3855 Shem's¦3857 tents¦3855. May Canaan¦3859 also¦3854 become a slave¦3860 of¦3855 Yafet.”
\p
\v 28 After¦3866 the¦3867 flood¦3867, Noah¦3865 lived¦3863 another 350 years¦3870,
\v 29 and¦3874 so he was 950 years¦3882 old when he died¦3885.
\c 10
\s1 Noah's grandchildren and their clans
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Noah's Sons; Nations Descended from Noah; The Table of Nations
\r (1 Chr. 1:5-23)
\p
\v 1 These¦3888 are¦3889 the generations of¦3889 Noah's¦3892 sons¦3890, Yafet¦3895 (Japheth), Shem¦3893, and Ham¦3894, and their descendants born¦3896 to¦3897 them¦3897 after¦3899 the flood¦3900:
\s2 Yafet's (Japheth's) descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Japheth
\rem /s2 The Japhethites
\p
\v 2 Yafet's (Japheth's¦3903) sons¦3902 were¦3904 Gomer¦3904, Magog¦3905, Madai¦3906, Yavan¦3907, Tubal¦3908, Meshech¦3909, and¦3905 Tiras¦3910.
\v 3 Gomer's¦3913 sons¦3912 were¦3914 Ashkenaz¦3914, Rifath¦3915, and¦3912 Togarmah¦3916.
\v 4 Yavan's¦3919 sons¦3918 were¦3920 Elishah¦3920 and Tarshish¦3921, and Kittim¦3922\f + \fr 10:1 \ft These names ending with ‘-im’ (the Hebrew plural) are possibly the names of the ensuing \+em tribes\+em* (e.g., ‘Kittites’) and not the individuals. (Similarly for v13-14.)\f* and Dodanim¦3923.
\v 5 From¦3925 \add all\add* those¦3925 \add descendants\add*, people spread out into areas near the coast¦3927, each¦3930 group with their own language¦3931, and with \add all\add* the clans¦3932 making up their nations¦3933.
\s2 Ham's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Ham
\rem /s2 The Hamites
\p
\v 6 Ham's¦3936 sons¦3935 were¦3937 Cush¦3937, Egypt¦3938 \add (Hebrew: Mitsrayim)\add*, Put¦3939, and¦3935 Canaan¦3940.
\v 7 Cush's¦3943 sons were Seba¦3944, Havilah¦3945, Sabtah¦3946, Raamah, and Sabtekah¦3948. Then¦3942 Raamah's¦3947 sons were Sheba¦3951 and Dedan¦3952.
\v 8 Cush was \add also\add* the father¦3955 of Nimrod¦3958 who became a mighty¦3962 \add warrior\add* on¦3963 the earth¦3963.
\v 9 Nimrod also became a terrific hunter \add noticed\add* by Yahweh, so¦3975 people said¦3976, “Like¦3977 Nimrod, a terrific hunter \add noticed\add* by Yahweh.”
\v 10 His¦3985 kingdom¦3985 began¦3984 in \add the cities of\add* Babel¦3986, Erech¦3987, Accad¦3988, and¦3983 Calneh¦3989, \add all\add* in the Shinar¦3991 region¦3990.
\v 11 From¦3993 that¦3996 area he \add extended\add* to Asshur¦3998\f + \fr 10:11 \ft Known to us as ‘Assyria’.\f* where he built¦3999 \add the cities of\add* Nineveh¦4002, Rehobot¦4005-Ir¦4006, Calah¦4009,
\v 12 and Resen¦4013, between¦4014 Nineveh¦4015 and the¦4019 large¦4020 city¦4019 of Calah¦4017.
\p
\v 13 Mitsrayim¦4022 fathered¦4023 Ludim¦4026, Anamim¦4029, Lehabim¦4032, Naftuhim¦4035,
\v 14 Pathrusim¦4039, and Casluhim¦4042 (who the¦4042 Philistines¦4046 came¦4044 from¦4045), and Caftorim¦4049.
\p
\v 15 Canaan¦4052 fathered¦4053 Tsidon¦4056 his¦4057 firstborn¦4057, then Het¦4060,\f + \fr 10:15 \ft The ancestor of the Hittites.\f*
\v 16 Yebusi¦4064, Emori¦4067, Girgashi¦4069.
\v 17 Hivi¦4073, Arqi¦4076, Sini¦4079,
\v 18 Arvadi¦4083, Tsemari¦4086, and¦4081 Hamati¦4089. Later¦4090 on, the¦4092 Canaan¦4093 clans¦4092 spread in all directions
\v 19 and their border¦4096 went¦4099 from¦4098 Sidon¦4098 toward Gerar¦4100 as far as Gaza¦4103, and then¦4095 toward Sodom¦4106, Amorah¦4106 \add !(Gomorrah¦4106)\add*, Admah¦4107, and Tsevoim, as far as Lasha¦4111.
\v 20 Those are Ham's¦4116 descendants according¦4118 to¦4117 their¦4117 clans¦4117, languages¦4118, regions, and nations¦4120.
\s2 Shem's descendants
\rem /s1 Descendants of Shem
\rem /s2 The Semites
\p
\v 21 Shem¦4123 (the younger brother¦4134 of Yafet¦4135) also¦4123 had \add some\add* sons¦4131 and¦4123 became the ancestor of all¦4129 the descendants of Eber¦4133.
\v 22 Shem's¦4139 sons¦4138 were¦4140 Eylam¦4140, Asshur¦4141, Arpachshad¦4142, Lud¦4143, and¦4142 Aram¦4144.
\v 23 Aram's¦4147 sons¦4146 were¦4148 Uz¦4148, Hul¦4149, Gether¦4150, and¦4146 Mash¦4151.
\v 24 Arpachshad fathered Shelah, then¦4153 Shelah fathered Eber¦4162.
\v 25 Eber's two¦4166 sons¦4167 were Peleg and¦4164 Yoktan¦4177. (Peleg's¦4170 name¦4168 \add means ‘division’,\add* because¦4171 it¦4173 was during his¦4176 lifetime that the¦4168 earth¦4174 was divided¦4173.\f + \fr 10:25 \ft Often believed to refer to the division of languages after the building of the tower described in chapter 11.\f*)
\v 26 Yoktan fathered¦4180 Almodad¦4183, Shelef¦4186, Hazarmaveth¦4189, Yerah¦4192,
\v 27 Hadoram¦4196, Uzal¦4199, Diklah¦4202,
\v 28 Obal¦4206, Abimael¦4209, Sheba¦4212,
\v 29 Ofir¦4216, Havilah¦4219, and¦4214 Yobab¦4222. (All¦4223 of those¦4225 were¦4226 the¦4226 sons¦4226 of Yoktan¦4227.)
\v 30 They lived in the area from¦4231 Mesha¦4231 then¦4229 to Sefar in the eastern¦4235 hills.
\v 31 Those are Shem's¦4240 descendants according to¦4241 their¦4241 clans¦4241, languages¦4242, regions, and nations¦4244.
\p
\v 32 All of those¦4253 are the clans¦4247 of Noah's¦4250 sons¦4248, according¦4251 to¦4251 their¦4251 generations and nations, and from¦4253 them the nations spread out across the earth¦4256 after¦4257 the flood¦4258.
\c 11
\s1 The tower at Babel
\rem /s1 The Tower of Babel; The Tower of Babylon
\p
\v 1 Now the whole¦4262 world had a single¦4266 language¦4265 and¦4267 the same words¦4267,
\v 2 and as they migrated from¦4272 the east¦4272, they discovered a plain¦4274 in¦4275 the Shinar¦4276 region¦4275 and settled there¦4278.
\v 3 Then¦4280 they said¦4280 to¦4282 each¦4281 other, “Come¦4285 on, let's¦4286 make¦4286 bricks and cure them¦4291 with fire.” So¦4280 they had bricks instead of stones¦4293, and they had tar for¦4296 mortar¦4297.
\p
\v 4 Then¦4299 they¦4299 said¦4299, “Come¦4300 on, let's build¦4302 a city¦4305 for ourselves and a tower¦4306 with its¦4307 top¦4307 in¦4308 the sky, and let's make¦4309 a reputation¦4312 for ourselves, so that we¦4315 don't get scattered¦4315 all¦4319 over¦4316 the whole¦4319 earth¦4321.”
\v 5 Then¦4323 Yahweh¦4324 came down to see¦4325 the city¦4328 and¦4329 the tower¦4331 that¦4332 the humans¦4335 were building¦4333
\v 6 and he¦4337 said¦4337, “Look, they're \add just\add* one¦4341 people¦4340 group, and they all¦4344 speak the same language¦4342. This¦4345 is only the beginning¦4346 of what they can do \add together\add*—soon they'll¦4355 be¦4351 capable of doing¦4356 anything they plan¦4355 to¦4344 do.
\v 7 Come¦4358 on then, let's¦4359 go¦4359 down¦4359 and¦4360 confuse¦4360 their¦4362 language¦4362 so that¦4363 they¦4365 won't¦4364 be able to understand¦4365 each¦4366 other's words.”
\v 8 So¦4372 then¦4370 they¦4380 stopped¦4380 building¦4381 the city¦4382 and¦4370 Yahweh¦4371 caused them¦4372 to¦4381 scatter¦4370 over¦4374 the whole¦4377 world.
\v 9 That's¦4401 why they named the city ‘Babel¦4389’ \add (meaning ‘mixed-up’)\add* because¦4390 it¦4400 was there¦4392 that Yahweh¦4394 mixed up everyone's language¦4395 and¦4399 then scattered¦4400 them¦4400 over¦4402 the whole¦4396 world.
\s1 Shem's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Shem; From Shem to Abram; Descendants of Shem
\r (1 Chr. 1:24-27)
\p
\v 10 This is the record of¦4411 Shem's¦4412 descendants: Two¦4422 years after¦4423 the flood¦4424, when Shem was¦4414 100 years old, he¦4418 had \add a son named\add* Arpakshad¦4421.
\v 11 After¦4429 Arpakshad¦4433 was born, Shem¦4428 lived¦4426 \add another\add* 500 years¦4436 and had \add other\add* sons¦4438 and daughters¦4439.
\p
\v 12 When Arpakshad¦4442 was thirty-five¦4445 years¦4446 old, he¦4443 had \add a son named\add* Shalah.
\v 13 After¦4454 Shalah¦4458 was born, Arpakshad¦4453 lived¦4452 \add another\add* 403 years¦4460 and had \add other\add* sons¦4465 and daughters¦4466.
\p
\v 14 When Shalah was thirty¦4471 years¦4472 old, he¦4470 had \add a son named\add* Eber¦4476.
\v 15 After¦4481 Eber¦4485 was born, Shelah¦4480 lived¦4478 \add another\add* 403 years¦4487 and had \add other\add* sons¦4492 and daughters¦4493.
\p
\v 16 When Eber¦4498 was thirty-four¦4500 years¦4501 old, he¦4496 had \add a son named\add* Peleg¦4505.
\v 17 After¦4510 Peleg¦4514 was born, Eber¦4509 lived¦4507 \add another\add* 430 years¦4516 and had \add other\add* sons¦4521 and daughters¦4522
\p
\v 18 When Peleg¦4527 was thirty¦4528 years¦4529 old, he¦4525 had \add a son named\add* Reu¦4533.
\v 19 After¦4538 Reu¦4542 was born, Peleg¦4537 lived¦4535 \add another\add* 209 years¦4544 and had \add other\add* sons¦4548 and daughters¦4549.
\p
\v 20 When Reu¦4553 was thirty-two¦4555 years¦4556 old, he¦4552 had \add a son named\add* Serug¦4560.
\v 21 After¦4564 Serug¦4568 was born, Reu¦4563 lived¦4562 \add another\add* 207 years¦4570 and had \add other\add* sons¦4574 and daughters¦4575.
\p
\v 22 When¦4583 Serug¦4579 was thirty¦4580 years¦4581 old, he¦4578 had \add a son named\add* Nahor¦4585.
\v 23 After¦4591 Nahor¦4593 was born, Serug¦4588 lived¦4587 \add another\add* 200 years¦4595 and had \add other\add* sons¦4597 and daughters¦4598.
\p
\v 24 When¦4607 Nahor¦4602 was twenty-nine¦4604 years¦4605 old, he¦4601 had \add a son named\add* Terah¦4609.
\v 25 After¦4615 Terah¦4617 was born, Nahor¦4612 lived¦4611 \add another\add* 119 years¦4621 and had \add other\add* sons¦4625 and daughters¦4626.
\p
\v 26 After Terah¦4631 was seventy¦4632 years¦4633 old, he¦4629 had \add sons whose names were\add* Abram¦4637, Nahor¦4640, and¦4641 Haran¦4643.
\s1 Terah's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Terah; Descendants of Terah; The Family of Terah
\p
\v 27 This is the¦4646 record of¦4646 Terah's descendants: Terah's sons were Abram¦4652, Nahor¦4655, and Haran and Haran had \add a son named\add* Lot¦4663.
\v 28 While¦4670 his¦4671 father¦4671 was still alive, Haran¦4666 died¦4665 in the land¦4672 where he¦4665 was born, in \add the city of\add* Ur¦4674 where the Chaldeans¦4675 lived.
\v 29 Meanwhile Abram¦4678 married Sarai¦4686 and Nahor married Milcah. (Milcah and \add her sister\add* Iscah¦4699 were the¦4682 daughters¦4692 of¦4682 \add Nahor's brother\add* Haran¦4694.)
\v 30 However, Sarai¦4702 wasn't able to¦4705 get pregnant \add so they\add* didn't¦4704 have any children¦4706.
\p
\v 31 Then¦4708 one day, Terah¦4709 gathered his son Abram and his grandson Lot¦4716 (who was Haran's¦4719 son), and his daughter-in-law¦4725 Sarai¦4724 (who was Abram's¦4712 wife¦4726), and they \add all\add* left from¦4731 \add the city of\add* Ur¦4731 (where the Chaldeans¦4732 lived¦4740), to travel to the region¦4734 of Canaan¦4735, \add but\add* when they arrived at \add the city of\add* Haran, they \add decided to\add* live there¦4741.
\v 32 Then¦4743 when Terah¦4746 was 205 years¦4748 old, he¦4751 \add died\add* there in¦4753 Haran¦4753.
\c 12
\s1 God commissions Abram to go
\rem /s1 Abram's travels; The Call of Abram; God's Call to Abram
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh¦4757 said¦4756 to Abram¦4760, “You must¦4767 leave your land and your relatives and your father's¦4767 house¦4766 and go¦4761 to the¦4770 land that¦4771 I'll¦4767 show¦4772 you.\x + \xo 12:1: \xt Acts 7:2-3; Heb 11:8.\x*
\v 2 I'll bless¦4777 you and make you into a great¦4776 nation¦4775. I'll make you famous and you'll be¦4780 a blessing¦4781 \add to others\add*.
\v 3 I'll bless¦4783 everyone¦4789 who blesses¦4783 you, but¦4783 I'll curse¦4786 anyone who curses¦4786 you. All¦4789 the families on the earth will¦4783 be¦4787 blessed¦4787 because of¦4789 you.\x + \xo 12:3: \xt Gal 3:8.\x*
\p
\v 4 So¦4793 Abram left \add there\add* just¦4795 as¦4795 Yahweh¦4798 had¦4796 told him, and¦4793 Lot¦4801 \add also\add* went¦4799 with¦4800 him. Abram was¦4803 seventy-five¦4807 years¦4806 old when he¦4796 left \add the city of\add* Haran¦4810.
\v 5 He¦4812 took¦4812 his wife¦4817 Sarai¦4816 and his nephew Lot¦4820 and all¦4826 their¦4828 possessions that¦4829 they had accumulated¦4830 and the people¦4833 who they had acquired¦4836 in¦4837 Haran¦4837, and they set out¦4838 to go¦4839 to the land¦4840 of¦4841 Canaan¦4841.
\p When¦4812 they¦4830 arrived in the¦4833 Canaan region¦4840,
\v 6 they continued travelling as far as the¦4848 city of¦4850 Shekem¦4851, to¦4852 Moreh's¦4854 oak tree¦4853. (The¦4850 Canaanites were still living in that region at that time.)
\v 7 Then¦4859 Yahweh came to Abram and told him, “I'll¦4866 give¦4866 this¦4870 land¦4869 to your¦4865 descendants.” So¦4859 Abram built¦4871 an¦4873 altar¦4873 there¦4872 \add and made a burnt offering\add* to Yahweh, who¦4875 had¦4875 appeared¦4875 to him.\x + \xo 12:7: \xt Acts 7:5; Gal 3:16.\x*
\v 8 Then¦4878 from¦4879 there he¦4878 went on to the hills to the east of¦4882 Beyt-El (Beyt-El¦4882) and pitched¦4885 his¦4886 tents¦4886, with Beyt-El on the west¦4891 and Ai¦4892 on the east. He¦4894 built¦4894 an¦4897 altar¦4897 to Yahweh there, and \add sacrificed and\add* prayed to Yahweh
\v 9 before continuing¦4906 \add south down\add* toward¦4907 the¦4907 Negev¦4904 \add desert\add*.
\s1 Abram goes to Egypt
\rem /s1 Abram in Egypt; Abram and Sarai in Egypt
\p
\v 10 \add One time when\add* there was¦4910 \add a drought causing\add* a severe food shortage, Abram¦4914 \add and his household\add* travelled \add west\add* across to Egypt¦4915 \add !(Mitsrayim¦4915)\add* to stay there.
\v 11 Just¦4925 before they arrived in Mitsrayim¦4928, \add Abram\add* said¦4929 to¦4927 his¦4933 wife¦4933 Sarai¦4932, “Listen, you're a very beautiful¦4940 woman¦4939,
\v 12 so when the¦4950 Egyptians¦4950 see¦4948 you they'll say¦4951, ‘This¦4953 is his¦4952 wife¦4952,’ and¦4945 they'll kill¦4954 me¦4955 but¦4945 let¦4957 you live¦4957.
\v 13 \add Please\add* tell them that¦4964 you're my¦4962 sister¦4962 so¦4964 they'll treat me well¦4965 because¦4968 of¦4968 you and¦4969 let me live¦4969.”\x + \xo 12:13: \xt Gen 20:2; 26:7.\x*
\v 14 So¦4973 that's¦4982 what happened when they¦4977 entered¦4974 Mitsrayim¦4976—the Egyptians \add indeed\add* noticed¦4977 that Sarai was¦4984 very¦4986 beautiful¦4984,
\v 15 and \add when\add* Far'oh's \add !(Pharaoh's¦4991)\add* officials¦4990 saw¦4988 her, so they¦4988 told him \add about her beauty\add*, and she¦4997 was¦4997 taken¦4997 into Far'oh's \add palace\add*.
\v 16 He¦5003 treated Abram¦5002 well \add (thinking he was the brother)\add*, so he \add was given\add* flocks and herds, male and female donkeys¦5011, male and female slaves, and camels¦5015.
\p
\v 17 \add But\add* Yahweh¦5018 directed terrible \add ≈deadly¦5023 diseases¦5023\add* onto Far'oh¦5022 and¦5025 his household¦5027 \add because of his taking\add* Abram's¦5033 wife¦5032 Sarai¦5031.
\v 18 So¦5035 Far'oh¦5036 called¦5035 for¦5043 Abram¦5037 and¦5035 said¦5038, “What's¦5039 this¦5041 you've done¦5042 to¦5043 me? Why didn't¦5045 you tell¦5047 me that¦5041 she's¦5051 your¦5050 wife¦5050?
\v 19 Why¦5062 did¦5054 you¦5054 say¦5054, ‘She's¦5056 my¦5055 sister¦5055,’ so that I¦5057 took¦5057 her in as a wife? Now¦5061 then, here's¦5062 your¦5063 wife. Take¦5064 her and¦5061 get out of¦5055 here.”
\v 20 Then¦5067 Far'oh¦5069 commanded¦5067 his \add officers\add*, and they¦5071 sent¦5071 Abram and his wife¦5075 away¦5071 and everything that¦5080 belonged to¦5082 him¦5082.
\c 13
\s1 Abram and Lot part ways
\rem /s1 Abram and Lot Separate
\p
\v 1 Then¦5084 Abram¦5085 left Egypt¦5086 with¦5095 his¦5088 wife¦5088 and everything that¦5091 belonged to him¦5093, and \add their nephew\add* Lot¦5094. \add They returned\add* to the¦5096 Negev
\v 2 and \add now\add* Abram had become very¦5100 wealthy¦5099, with¦5101 \add both\add* livestock¦5101, and silver¦5102 and gold¦5103.
\v 3 Then¦5105 they continued \add north\add* through the Negev¦5107 as far as Beyt-El, to¦5113 the place¦5115 where¦5116 his¦5120 tents¦5120 had¦5118 been¦5118 at¦5122 the beginning¦5122—between Beyt-El and¦5105 Ai¦5128—
\v 4 to the¦5132 place¦5132 where he'd previously built an altar¦5133,\x + \xo 13:4: \xt Gen 12:8.\x* and Abram¦5141 \add sacrificed and\add* prayed to Yahweh¦5143 there¦5140.
\p
\v 5 Now Lot¦5147, who¦5148 was¦5148 travelling¦5148 with¦5149 Abram¦5151, also¦5145 had flocks and herds¦5155 and tents¦5156,
\v 6 but¦5158 the land¦5162 couldn't support¦5160 them¦5161 \add all\add* while they stayed¦5163 together¦5173. Because¦5161 \add the needs of\add* their¦5168 combined assets were¦5171 so \add demanding\add*, they couldn't all \add thrive\add* in one place
\v 7 and that led to strife between¦5178 Abram's¦5182 and Lot's¦5187 herdsmen¦5184. Also¦5175, the Canaanites¦5188 and the Perizzites¦5189 were living¦5191 in the land at that time.
\p
\v 8 So¦5194 Abram¦5195 said¦5194 to¦5196 Lot¦5198, “Let's¦5202 not¦5199 have quarrelling between you¦5205 and I, or between yours herdsmen¦5207 and mine, because¦5210 we're \add relatives\add*.
\v 9 Isn't the whole¦5217 land¦5219 available to you? So let's separate¦5221. If¦5224 you go to the left then I'll go right, or if¦5228 you go to the right then I'll go left.”
\v 10 Lot¦5235 looked \add all around\add* and saw¦5239 that¦5246 all the plain of the Yordan¦5245 \add river valley\add* was¦5248 well-watered¦5248 through towards Tso'ar, \add just\add* like Yahweh's¦5252 garden¦5259 \add had been\add* before¦5249 he¦5233 destroyed¦5251 Sodom¦5255 and Amorah¦5258 \add !(Gomorrah¦5258)\add*, and like the land¦5262 of Egypt¦5263.\x + \xo 13:10: \xt Gen 2:10.\x*
\v 11 Lot¦5270 chose¦5267 all¦5272 of¦5272 the Yordan¦5275 plain for himself, and so he¦5276 travelled to¦5269 the east¦5278 and they¦5279 separated¦5279 from¦5281 each¦5272 other.
\v 12 \add So\add* Abram¦5284 settled in the land¦5286 of Canaan¦5288, and Lot¦5289 settled in the cities¦5291 of the plain¦5292 and moved¦5293 his tents¦5293 as far as Sodom¦5296.
\v 13 \add However,\add* the¦5298 people¦5298 of¦5298 Sodom¦5299 were¦5300 extremely wicked¦5300, \add constantly\add* disobeying Yahweh¦5302.
\s1 God promises Abram the land
