\v 8 Then¦11510 when he'd been in that area for¦11511 quite a while, the¦11516 Philistine¦11520 King¦11519 Abimelech¦11518 looked¦11517 down¦11517 from a window¦11522 and¦11510 saw¦11523 that \add to his surprise\add*, Yitshak¦11525 was¦11526 laughing \add together\add* with¦11527 his wife¦11529 Rebekah¦11528.
\v 9 So Abimelech¦11532 called¦11531 for Yitshak¦11533 and¦11531 said¦11534, “Hey, she's¦11538 really your¦11537 wife¦11537! So how¦11539 could you¦11540 say¦11540, ‘She's¦11538 my¦11541 sister¦11541’?”
\p “Because¦11546 I said \add to myself\add*, ‘I might die¦11550 because¦11546 of¦11537 her¦11551.’,” Yitshak replied¦11534.
\p
\v 10 “What's¦11555 this¦11557 that¦11557 you've done¦11558 to¦11559 us?” continued¦11553 Abimelech¦11554. “One¦11562 of¦11563 \add my\add* people¦11563 might¦11560 easily have slept with¦11564 your wife¦11566 and¦11567 you would have brought¦11567 condemnation onto us!”
\v 11 Then¦11571 \add King\add* Abimelech¦11572 ordered¦11571 all¦11575 the¦11577 people¦11577, “Anyone¦11579 who¦11579 touches¦11579 this¦11581 man¦11580 or his¦11582 wife¦11582 will¦11584 certainly be¦11584 killed.”
\rem /s1 Conflict over Water Rights
\p
\v 12 Then¦11586 Yitshak¦11587 \add planted a crop\add* in¦11591 that¦11589 land and Yahweh¦11596 blessed¦11595 him and gave him one¦11593 hundred¦11593 times as much as he'd planted.
\v 13 Hence¦11604 he became¦11606 \add wealthier\add* and¦11598 that repeated until¦11603 \add eventually\add* he was \add very powerful\add*.
\v 14 He¦11609 \add also\add* owned sheep and goats, and cattle, and many slaves¦11617. \add Because\add* the¦11621 Philistines¦11621 \add now\add* envied¦11619 him¦11620,
\v 15 they blocked up¦11633 all¦11623 the¦11625 wells¦11625 that¦11626 his father's¦11629 slaves¦11628 had dug¦11627 when his father Abraham¦11634 had been alive, and¦11623 they filled¦11635 them¦11633 with¦11630 dirt.
\p
\v 16 Then¦11638 \add King\add* Abimelech¦11639 told Yitshak¦11642, “You¦11647 all \add need to\add* leave this area because¦11645 you've¦11647 become¦11647 more powerful¦11647 than us¦11644.”
\v 17 So¦11652 Yitshak¦11654 left that place and set up camp in¦11656 the¦11656 Gerar¦11658 Valley and settled there¦11660.
\v 18 Then¦11662 he returned¦11662 and dug out the water¦11669 wells¦11668 that they¦11675 had dug in¦11672 the days¦11672 of¦11672 Abraham his father and that the Philistines¦11676 had blocked up¦11675 after¦11677 Abraham's¦11673 death¦11678, and he called them similar names¦11682 to¦11681 the ones that his father had called them.
\p
\v 19 Then¦11690 Yitshak's¦11693 slaves¦11691 dug¦11690 in¦11694 the¦11691 valley¦11694 and¦11695 found¦11695 an \add underground\add* spring with¦11694 flowing water¦11699 there¦11697.
\v 20 But¦11702 the herdsmen from Gerar¦11704 quarrelled¦11718 with Yitshak's¦11708 herdsmen, saying¦11709, “The¦11703 water¦11711 belongs to¦11709 us¦11710.” So¦11702 he¦11712 named the well¦11715 ‘Esek¦11716’ \add (which means ‘dispute’)\add*, because¦11717 they¦11718 disputed¦11702 with him¦11719.
