\p
\v 1 When Yitshak¦11954 grew¦11955 old¦11953 and could no longer see¦11957, he¦11950 called¦11958 his¦11956 older son Esaw¦11961 and said¦11965 to¦11966 him¦11966, “My son.”
\p “\add I'm here\add*,” Esaw replied¦11965.
\p
\v 2 “Please¦11975 listen,” Yitshak \add continued\add*, “I'm¦11976 old¦11976 and¦11972 don't¦11977 know¦11978 how long I've got before I die.
\v 3 So¦11982 please¦11985 take¦11983 your weapons¦11986—your quiver¦11987, and your bow¦11988—and go¦11989 out¦11989 into the¦11990 countryside¦11990 and hunt¦11991 game¦11993 for¦11992 me¦11992
\v 4 and cook me a tasty meal in¦12008 the way that I love¦12001 it, and bring¦12002 it to¦12003 me so I can eat¦12004 it, so that \add I\add* can bless¦12006 you¦12006 before¦12008 I die¦12009.”
\p
\v 5 \add Now\add* Rebekah¦12011 had been listening¦12012 while Yitshak¦12014 spoke¦12013 to Esaw. Then¦12011 Esaw went¦12019 \add out\add* to the¦12021 countryside¦12021 to hunt¦12022 game¦12023 and¦12019 bring¦12024 it¦12019 back.
\v 6 \add Meanwhile\add* Rebekah¦12026 said¦12027 to her¦12031 son¦12031 Yacob¦12030, “Listen, I¦12034 heard¦12034 your father¦12037 speaking¦12038 to your brother¦12042 Esaw¦12041, saying¦12027,
\v 7 ‘Get \add some\add* game¦12047 \add meat\add* for¦12046 me and prepare¦12048 me a tasty meal so¦12052 that¦12052 I can eat¦12052 it, and \add then\add* I can bless¦12053 you¦12053 in¦12054 Yahweh's¦12055 presence before¦12054 I die¦12057.’
\v 8 So¦12059 now¦12059 \add Yacob\add*, listen¦12061 to¦12062 what I¦12064 tell you¦12066:
\v 9 Go¦12068 \add out\add* to¦12071 the flock¦12073 and¦12074 get me¦12076 two¦12078 of¦12078 the \add best\add* young goats¦12080 so¦12082 that I¦12082 can prepare¦12082 a tasty meal from¦12077 them¦12083 that your¦12085 father¦12085 loves¦12087.
\v 10 Then¦12089 you \add can\add* take¦12089 it \add in\add* to¦12090 your¦12090 father¦12090, and¦12091 \add after\add* he's¦12091 eaten¦12091 it, he'll¦12091 bless¦12094 you before¦12095 he dies¦12096.”
\p
\v 11 “Look,” Yacob¦12099 said¦12098 to¦12100 his¦12103 mother¦12103, “My¦12106 brother¦12106 Esaw¦12105 is \add very\add* hairy¦12108, whereas \add my skin\add* is smooth¦12111.
\v 12 What if my¦12115 father¦12115 touches me? Then he'll think of¦12115 me as¦12118 a deceiver¦12118 and¦12119 \add he'll\add* curse¦12121 me rather than bless¦12123 me.”
\p
\v 13 “Let your¦12129 curse¦12129 be¦12128 on¦12133 me, my¦12133 son¦12130,” his¦12127 mother¦12127 \add ≈replied¦12125\add*, “Just \add do\add* what I said¦12125 and¦12134 go¦12134 get \add the young goats\add* for me.”
\v 14 \add So\add* he¦12139 went¦12139 and \add slaughtered them\add* and brought¦12141 \add them\add* to¦12142 his mother¦12144. Then¦12139 \add she\add* prepared¦12143 tasty food¦12145 the way his father¦12148 loved¦12147 \add it\add*.
\v 15 \add She then\add* got Esaw's¦12155 best clothes¦12154 that¦12159 were¦12160 in¦12161 the¦12154 house¦12161 and¦12162 \add got\add* her¦12156 younger¦12167 son¦12156 Yacob¦12165 to put \add them\add* on¦12161,
\v 16 and then¦12169 she¦12173 \add tied\add* the skins¦12170 of the young goats¦12172 \add over\add* his hands¦12176 and¦12177 \add around\add* the smooth¦12178 part¦12178 of his neck¦12179.
\p
\v 17 Then¦12181 she¦12189 \add handed\add* the tasty¦12184 food¦12187 and¦12181 the bread¦12187 to Yacob¦12191,
\v 18 and he¦12194 went¦12194 \add in\add* to¦12195 his¦12197 \add elderly\add* father and said¦12198, “My father.”
\p “I'm¦12201 here¦12201,” \add he\add* said, “Which son¦12204 are¦12203 you¦12203?”
\v 19 “I'm¦12211 your¦12213 oldest son Esaw¦12212,” Yacob¦12207 \add ≈replied¦12206\add* to¦12208 his¦12210 father¦12210. “I've¦12214 done¦12214 what you told me. Please¦12220 get up, \add and come and\add* sit¦12221 up and eat¦12222 some¦12223 of¦12223 my¦12223 game¦12223, so that you'll bless¦12225 me.”
\v 20 “How did you¦12236 find¦12237 it¦12228 so quickly, my son¦12232?” Yitshak¦12229 \add asked\add*.
\p “Because¦12240 your¦12243 god¦12243 Yahweh¦12242 \add helped me when I went out hunting\add*,” Yacob \add ≈answered¦12228\add*.
\p
\v 21 Then¦12246 Yitshak¦12247 said¦12246 to Yacob¦12250, “Please¦12253 come¦12251 close¦12251 so¦12254 that¦12257 I¦12254 can touch you, my son, and¦12246 \add to be sure\add* whether¦12256 you're really my son Esaw¦12259 or¦12260 not¦12262.”
\v 22 So¦12264 Yacob went close¦12264 to his father¦12269 and \add Yitshak\add* felt¦12270 \add his skin\add* and said¦12271 \add to himself\add*, “It's¦12271 Yacob's¦12265 voice¦12272, but¦12264 \add these\add* are¦12276 \add definitely\add* Esaw's¦12277 hands¦12276.”
\v 23 So¦12279 he didn't¦12279 recognise¦12280 \add that it was Yacob\add*, because¦12281 his¦12284 hands were¦12283 hairy¦12288 like¦12285 Esaw's¦12286 hands, so he was about¦12284 to bless¦12289 him¦12289
\v 24 \add but asked once more,\add* “Are¦12292 you¦12292 really my¦12294 son¦12294 Esaw¦12295?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* I¦12297 am,” Yacob answered¦12291.
\p
\v 25 \add So Yitshak told him\add*, “Bring¦12300 the¦12303 food \add here\add* and I'll eat¦12302 some¦12303 of¦12304 my¦12307 son's¦12304 game¦12303, so¦12305 that¦12305 \add I can then\add* bless¦12306 you¦12306.” \add So Yacob\add* took it¦12306 to him and he ate¦12311, and then he brought¦12312 wine¦12314 to him and he drank¦12315.
\v 26 Then¦12317 Yitshak¦12319 asked \add Yacob\add*, “My son¦12327, please¦12323 come¦12321 close¦12321 and¦12324 kiss¦12324 me¦12326.”
\v 27 So¦12329 he went¦12329 close¦12329 and¦12329 kissed¦12330 him. \add His dad noticed\add* the¦12336 smell¦12336 of¦12343 his clothes¦12337, so he blessed¦12338 him saying¦12339,\x + \xo 27:27-29: \xt Heb 11:20.\x*
\q1 “\add Ah yes,\add* the smell of my son¦12342
