\v 34 “Okay¦14190 then¦14188, let's¦14192 \add do it your way\add*.” Lavan¦14189 \add agreed\add*.
\v 35 \add But\add* that \add very\add* day¦14196, he removed¦14195 the male¦14200 goats that were streaked¦14201 and spotted, and all the female¦14206 goats that were speckled¦14207 and spotted, any that had white¦14212 on¦14217 it¦14213, and all the dark-coloured ones among¦14217 the lambs¦14217. Then¦14195 he gave¦14218 them to his sons¦14221 \add to look after\add*,
\v 36 and¦14223 \add they took them\add* a three-day¦14225 journey¦14224 \add away from where\add* Yacob \add was\add*, \add so\add* Yacob was \add left just\add* tending \em the¦14234 rest\em* of¦14234 Lavan's¦14235 flocks¦14234.
\p
\v 37 Then¦14238 Yacob¦14241 \add got some\add* freshly¦14244 cut branches of¦14242 poplar¦14243 and almond¦14245 and plane¦14246 trees¦14246, and peeled¦14248 white stripes¦14250 in¦14249 them¦14249 \add by\add* exposing the white that¦14254 was¦14255 inside the branches.
\v 38 Then¦14260 he put the branches¦14262 that¦14263 he had¦14264 peeled in¦14265 the water¦14267 troughs¦14265 where the flocks¦14270 \add would see them when they\add* came to drink, and¦14259 they mated when they came to drink.
\v 39 \add Thus\add* the flocks¦14279 would mate by the \add striped\add* branches¦14282, and \add they\add* would bear¦14283 young who were¦14278 streaked, speckled¦14286, and spotted¦14287.
\p
\v 40 Then¦14289 Yacob¦14291 \add separated out those young animals into a separate flock\add*, and he made¦14303 the \add older\add* flocks look at¦14301 the streaked and all¦14298 the dark-coloured animals in¦14301 Lavan's flocks. \add So\add* he kept his own flocks by themselves and didn't¦14308 \add mix\add* them¦14309 with¦14301 Lavan's flocks.
