\p
\v 1 \add One day, Yacob\add* overheard the¦14359 words¦14359 of¦14360 Lavan's¦14362 sons¦14360 who were saying¦14363, “Yacob has taken¦14364 everything that¦14371 belonged to our father, and¦14356 from¦14371 what \add originally\add* belonged to our father he¦14373 has gained all¦14375 this¦14378 wealth¦14377.”
\v 2 Then¦14380 Yacob¦14381 \add also noticed\add* that Lavan's¦14385 \add attitude towards him had changed\add*, and¦14386 he wasn't \add in favour of him like he'd been in the past\add*.
\v 3 Then¦14392 Yahweh¦14393 \add ≈told\add* Yacob¦14396, “Go back to the¦14400 land¦14400 of¦14400 your ancestors¦14401 and to your relatives, and I'll¦14403 be¦14403 with¦14404 you¦14404.”
\p
\v 4 \add So\add* Yacob¦14407 sent¦14406 for Rahel¦14409 and¦14406 Le'ah¦14410 to \add come to him out in\add* the¦14411 countryside¦14411 \add where he was with\add* his¦14414 flocks¦14414
\v 5 and he told them¦14417, “I've¦14419 \add noticed\add* your¦14423 father's \add attitude\add* and that¦14424 he's¦14426 not¦14426 \add positive\add* toward me \add like he was a few days\add* ago¦14429, but¦14416 my father's god¦14430 has¦14432 been¦14432 with¦14433 me.
\v 6 You \add both\add* know¦14436 that¦14437 I've¦14441 served¦14441 your¦14444 father¦14444 with all¦14438 my¦14440 strength¦14440,
\v 7 \add but\add* your¦14446 father¦14446 has¦14447 deceived¦14447 me and¦14446 changed¦14449 my¦14452 wages¦14452 ten¦14453 times¦14454. However¦14450, God¦14458 hasn't allowed¦14457 him¦14457 to¦14459 harm¦14459 me.
\v 8 \add When he told me\add*: ‘The speckled animals will be your wages,’ then all the flocks bore speckled young. But¦14469 when he \add ≈told me\add*: ‘The streaked ones will be your wages,’ then all the flocks bore streaked young.
\v 9 \add In that way,\add* God¦14488 took¦14487 your¦14492 father's¦14492 animals away¦14487 from him and¦14493 gave¦14493 them to¦14495 me¦14495.
\p
\v 10 “\add One day during\add* the season when the flocks were \add mating\add*, I¦14501 \add looked up\add* and saw¦14503 in¦14498 a dream¦14504 that, wow, the male¦14506 goats¦14506 that were mounting the flocks were streaked, speckled¦14512, and spotted.
\v 11 Then¦14515 God's messenger¦14517 said to¦14516 me¦14516 in¦14519 the¦14517 dream¦14519, ‘Yacob¦14520!’ and¦14515 I said, ‘\add I'm listening\add*.’
\v 12 Then¦14524 he¦14524 \add ≈told me\add*, ‘Please¦14527 \add look up\add* and \add observe that\add* all¦14530 the male¦14532 goats¦14532 that are mounting the flocks¦14536 are streaked, speckled¦14538, and spotted, because¦14540 I¦14541 have¦14541 seen¦14541 everything that Lavan¦14546 is¦14547 doing¦14547 to¦14548 you¦14548.
\v 13 I'm¦14550 the god¦14551 of¦14552 Beyt¦14552-El, where you anointed¦14556 a pillar¦14558—where you vowed¦14560 a vow¦14563 to me¦14561. Now¦14564 \add pack\add* up and depart from¦14567 this¦14570 land, and return¦14571 to the land where you were born.’ ”\x + \xo 31:13: \xt Gen 28:18-22.\x*
\p
\v 14 Then¦14577 Rahel¦14578 and¦14577 Le'ah¦14579 replied¦14580, “\add Yes,\add* we're not expecting any portion or inheritance¦14585 from our¦14587 father's¦14587 \add property\add*.
\v 15 Doesn't he just \add treat\add* us like foreigners¦14590 \add now\add*? \add Yes,\add* he sold¦14594 us and¦14595 then he \add frittered away all the money that should have been\add* ours,
\v 16 so all¦14604 the¦14606 wealth¦14606 that God took¦14608 from¦14610 our father¦14610 \add belonged\add* to us¦14611 and to our children¦14613 \add anyway\add*. So \add yes\add*, \add go ahead and\add* do everything that God's¦14618 told you¦14619 \add to do\add*.”
\p
\v 17 So¦14622 Yacob¦14623 \add packed\add* up¦14622 and put his¦14627 wives¦14630 and children¦14627 on¦14631 the¦14633 camels¦14633.
\v 18 Then¦14635 he drove¦14635 all his livestock and¦14635 all his property¦14645 that he had acquired—the livestock in his possession¦14649 that he had acquired in Paddan¦14652-Aram—to go¦14654 \add back\add* to Isaac¦14657 his father¦14658 in the Canaan¦14660 region¦14659.
\v 19 \add Now\add* Lavan had¦14663 gone¦14663 \add off for several days\add* to shear¦14664 his¦14667 sheep¦14667, so Rahel¦14669 stole¦14668 the¦14672 idols that¦14673 belonged¦14674 to her¦14674 father¦14674,
\v 20 and¦14676 Yacob¦14677 \add deceived\add* Lavan¦14681 (the¦14680 Syrian¦14682) by not¦14685 telling¦14686 him¦14687 that¦14688 \add they all\add* were leaving.
\v 21 \add So\add* Yacob \add took his household\add* and everything that¦14696 belonged to¦14698 \add them\add* and crossed the \add Euphrates\add* River¦14703 and \add headed upward\add* toward the hill-country of¦14708 Gilead¦14709.
\s1 Lavan catches up to Yacob
\rem /s1 Laban Overtakes Jacob; Laban Pursues Jacob
\p
\v 22 Three days¦14713 later, Lavan¦14712 was¦14711 \add informed\add* that¦14715 Yacob¦14717 had¦14716 fled¦14716 \add with his family\add*,
\v 23 \add so\add* he¦14719 took¦14719 \add some of\add* his relatives¦14722 with¦14731 him¦14725 and chased after¦14725 Yacob \add and his family\add* for seven¦14727 days¦14728 until they overtook¦14729 \add them\add* in¦14731 the¦14731 hill-country of Gilead¦14732.
\v 24 \add But\add* that night¦14741 God¦14735 \add appeared\add* to Lavan¦14738 the¦14739 Syrian¦14739 in¦14740 a dream¦14740 and¦14742 told him¦14743, “Watch yourself¦14745 that you¦14748 don't \add say anything to\add* Yacob¦14751, either good¦14752 or bad¦14755.”
\v 25 \add When\add* Lavan had caught up to Yacob, Yacob had pitched \add their\add* tents¦14766 in¦14767 the hills, so Lavan with¦14767 his¦14766 relatives¦14772 \add also\add* pitched theirs on¦14767 the Gilead¦14774 hills.
\p
\v 26 Then¦14776 Lavan¦14777 said¦14776 to¦14778 Yacob¦14778, “What¦14779 have¦14780 you¦14780 done¦14780? \add You've deceived me\add* and¦14776 carried¦14785 away¦14785 my¦14784 daughters¦14788 like¦14789 \add prisoners of war\add*.
\v 27 Why did¦14793 you flee¦14794 secretly and deceive¦14795 me and not¦14797 tell¦14799 me? I¦14801 would have sent¦14801 you \add all off\add* with¦14802 \add music and singing and a cheery party\add*.
\v 28 You¦14808 didn't¦14807 let me¦14808 kiss¦14809 my grandchildren \add goodbye\add* and¦14811 my daughters¦14811, \add instead you made a foolish decision\add*.
\v 29 \add I have the right and the ability to punish\add* you¦14821, but¦14823 last¦14825 night¦14825 your¦14824 father's¦14824 god¦14823 told me¦14828, ‘\add Be careful not to\add* speak¦14832 either good¦14836 or bad¦14839 to Yacob¦14835.’
\v 30 \add So\add* now¦14841, you probably left because¦14844 you miss your father's¦14849 place badly, \add but\add* why did¦14851 you steal¦14851 my¦14854 gods¦14854?”
\p
\v 31 “\add We left like that,\add*” Yacob¦14857 answered¦14856 Lavan¦14859, “because I was¦14861 afraid¦14861, because I said¦14863 that perhaps you¦14866 would \add use force to take\add* your¦14869 \add married\add* daughters¦14869 from¦14870 me¦14870.
\v 32 \add As for your idols,\add* anyone¦14873 you find¦14874 your gods¦14877 with will¦14879 \add be put to death\add*. Look through everything we have and¦14887 take¦14887 \add back anything that\add* belongs to you, with our¦14881 relatives¦14881 \add as witnesses\add*.” (Now Yacob¦14893 didn't¦14878 know¦14892 that Rahel¦14895 had¦14896 stolen¦14896 them¦14896.)
\p
\v 33 \add So\add* Lavan¦14899 \add inspected\add* Yacob's¦14901 and Le'ah's tents¦14900, and the tent of the two¦14906 slave women, but¦14898 he didn't¦14908 find¦14909 anything. Then¦14898 he came¦14910 out¦14910 of Le'ah's tent and went¦14913 into Rahel's¦14915 tent.
\v 34 Now Rahel had¦14918 taken¦14918 the idols and¦14917 put¦14922 them in¦14923 the camel's¦14924 saddle¦14923 \add which she was now\add* sitting¦14925 on¦14923, \add so although\add* Lavan¦14928 searched¦14927 throughout \add her\add* entire¦14931 tent¦14933, he¦14927 didn't¦14934 find¦14935 them,
\v 35 and¦14937 she¦14937 said¦14937 to her¦14940 father¦14940, “Don't¦14941 let¦14943 my¦14945 master¦14945 \add be upset\add* that I'm¦14948 not¦14947 able¦14948 to stand up in¦14944 your¦14950 presence¦14950, because¦14946 \add I have the regular female concern at the moment\add*.” So¦14937 he searched¦14956, but¦14937 he didn't¦14941 find¦14958 the idols.
\p
\v 36 \add So\add* Yacob¦14964 \add got very angry\add* and¦14963 he¦14965 argued with¦14966 Lavan¦14966, demanding from \add him\add*, “What's my crime? What's my sin¦14975, that you've¦14977 hotly¦14977 chased after¦14978 me¦14978?
\v 37 Since you've¦14982 searched¦14982 through all¦14985 \add our\add* things, what¦14988 things from¦14991 \em your\em* house¦14994 have¦14982 you found¦14990? Put¦14995 it here in front¦14997 of my¦14998 relatives and your relatives, and let them judge between¦15001 the¦15002 two¦15002 of us¦15002!
\p
\v 38 “I've¦15016 \add worked\add* for you¦15008 for twenty¦15005 years¦15006. Your¦15009 ewes¦15009 and your female¦15010 goats¦15010 didn't¦15011 miscarry¦15012, and I haven't eaten¦15016 rams¦15013 from your flocks¦15014.
\v 39 I didn't¦15019 bring¦15021 \add dead sheep or goats\add* to¦15022 you¦15022 \add that had been attacked by wild animals\add*—I bore¦15024 the loss¦15024 of¦15024 \add those\add* myself. You¦15026 required¦15026 \add that I cover anything\add* stolen¦15027 during the day¦15028 or during the night¦15030.
\v 40 I was¦15032 \add always out there\add*—during the day¦15033 the heat¦15035 \add tormented\add* me¦15034, and¦15036 at¦15033 night¦15037 the frost¦15036 \add so bad that I couldn't sleep\add*.
\v 41 That's how it was for me¦15044 for twenty¦15045 years in¦15047 your house¦15047. I¦15048 \add worked for you for\add* fourteen years for your two¦15053 daughters¦15054, then six¦15055 \add more\add* years for your flocks¦15057, and¦15058 you changed¦15058 my¦15061 wages¦15061 ten¦15062 times¦15063 \add to your advantage\add*.
\v 42 If¦15074 my father's¦15067 god—the god of¦15068 Abraham¦15069 and the one Yitshak¦15071 respected—had¦15072 not¦15065 been¦15072 with me, surely you would have sent¦15077 me away¦15077 empty-handed¦15076 now¦15075. God¦15066 has¦15086 seen¦15086 my suffering and \add my hard work\add*, and last¦15089 night¦15089, he¦15088 rebuked¦15088 you.”
\s1 Yacob and Lavan make a treaty
\rem /s1 Jacob's Treaty with Laban; Laban and Jacob Make a Covenant; The Agreement between Jacob and Laban
\p
\v 43 Then¦15091 Lavan¦15092 responded¦15093 and told Yacob¦15096, “\add These women\add* are my daughters, and the children are my grandchildren, and the flocks are my flocks. Everything¦15103 that you¦15106 see¦15107 \add here\add* belongs to me¦15108. But¦15091 what¦15112 can I¦15114 do¦15114 today about these¦15115 daughters of¦15111 mine or¦15117 about their¦15118 children that they¦15120 gave birth to?
\v 44 \add So\add* come¦15123 now¦15122, let's¦15124 make¦15124 an agreement¦15125, I¦15126 and you, and let it¦15128 be a witness¦15129 between¦15130 me¦15130 and you.”
\p
\v 45 So¦15133 Yacob¦15134 took¦15133 a stone¦15135 and¦15133 \add stood it up longways\add* as a pillar¦15137,
\v 46 then \add he instructed\add* his¦15141 relatives¦15141, “Gather¦15140 stones.” So¦15139 they \add fetched\add* stones and¦15139 made¦15146 a pile, then they ate¦15149 there¦15150 by the¦15153 pile.
\v 47 Lavan¦15158 gave the pile \add the Aramaic name\add* ‘Yegar¦15159 Sahadutha¦15160’ \add (which means ‘pile that reminds’)\add* while Yacob¦15161 gave it¦15155 \add the Hebrew name\add* ‘Gale'ed’ \add (which has the same meaning)\add*.
\p
\v 48 Then¦15166 Lavan¦15167 said¦15166, “This¦15169 pile is¦15170 a witness¦15170 \add of the agreement\add* between¦15171 me¦15171 and¦15166 you¦15172 today.” (That's why he'd named it¦15166 Galeed¦15181.)
\v 49 It's also¦15189 \add named\add* ‘Mitspah¦15183’ \add (which means ‘watchtower’)\add*, because¦15190 he¦15185 said¦15185, “May¦15186 Yahweh¦15187 watch¦15186 between¦15188 me¦15188 and¦15189 you¦15189 when we¦15191 are hidden¦15191 one from¦15193 the¦15183 other¦15193.
\v 50 If¦15198 you \add mistreat\add* my daughters, or if¦15201 you take¦15203 \add other\add* wives¦15204 besides my daughters, even though there's¦15208 not¦15208 someone¦15209 \add there to see it\add*, listen, God¦15212 \add will be\add* a witness¦15213 between¦15214 me¦15214 and¦15215 you.”
\v 51 Then¦15217 Lavan¦15218 said¦15217 to¦15219 Yacob¦15219, “Look at this¦15223 pile \add of stones\add*, and look at the¦15225 pillar¦15225 which¦15227 I¦15228 set¦15228 up¦15228 between¦15229 me¦15229 and you¦15230.
\v 52 This¦15242 pile is a witness and the¦15236 pillar¦15236 is a witness that I¦15239 won't go past these to you, and that you won't go past these to me¦15254 to do harm¦15263.
\v 53 May¦15269 Abraham's¦15266 god¦15265 and¦15267 the gods of¦15267 their¦15272 ancestor Nahor¦15268 judge¦15269 between¦15270 us¦15270.” Then Yacob¦15274 swore¦15273 by the one respected by his¦15276 father¦15276 Yitshak¦15277,
\v 54 and Yacob¦15280 offered a sacrifice¦15279 on the hill. Then¦15279 he¦15283 called¦15283 his¦15284 relatives¦15284 to¦15285 eat¦15285 bread and they¦15288 ate¦15288 bread and spent¦15290 the night¦15290 on the hill.
\v 55 In the morning, Lavan got up early and \add gave\add* his grandchildren and his daughters \add a kiss\add*, and he blessed them. Then \add he\add* left and returned to his place.
\c 32
\s1 Yacob encounters God's messengers
\rem /s1 Jacob Prepares to Meet Esau; Jacob Sends Gifts to Esau
\p
\v 1 Then Yacob \add continued on\add* his way \add with his family and flocks\add*, and \add some\add* of¦15305 God's messengers met him
\v 2 and¦15308 when he saw them, Yacob¦15308 said, “This \add must be\add* God's¦15315 army camp.” So he named that place ‘Mahanaim’ \add (which means ‘two camps’)\add*.
\s1 Yacob sends gifts ahead for Esaw
\rem /s1 Jacob Sends Presents to Appease Esau
\p
\v 3 Then Yacob¦15319 sent messengers ahead of him to his brother Esaw in the Se'ir region in the country of Edom,
\v 4 instructing them, “This is what you'll say to my master, to Esaw¦15340: ‘This is what your servant Yacob¦15335 says, “I have been staying with \add Uncle\add* Lavan and¦15334 have remained there until now.
\v 5 \add Now\add* I have¦15362 cattle and donkeys, flocks, and male and female slaves. And¦15348 I've¦15362 sent \add these messengers\add* to¦15350 speak with¦15359 my¦15353 master¦15353, so¦15351 that I'll find favour in your¦15357 eyes.” ’ ”
\p
\v 6 \add In due course\add* the messengers returned to Yacob, saying, “We went to your¦15383 brother Esaw and¦15369 \add now\add* he's¦15369 coming to meet you along with¦15383 his four hundred men!”
\v 7 \add This made\add* Yacob¦15390 very scared and distressed, so he¦15400 divided the people who were with¦15406 him¦15406 and the flocks and the herds and the camels into two groups,
\v 8 \add reasoning\add*, “If Esaw comes and¦15409 attacks one camp, then the¦15417 camp that's¦15418 left can escape.”
\p
\v 9 Then \add he prayed\add*, “Yahweh, God of my grandfather Abraham, and God of my father Yitshak, who said¦15432 to me, ‘Return to your land and to your relatives, and I will¦15435 cause things to prosper with you,’
\v 10 I'm unworthy of all the kindnesses and¦15448 of all the faithfulness that¦15457 you¦15463 have shown \add me\add* your¦15460 slave, because I crossed this Yordan \add River\add* with¦15463 \add only\add* my staff, but¦15448 now I've become two camps.
\v 11 Please save me from¦15467 my¦15478 brother Esaw, because¦15477 I'm¦15466 afraid that¦15472 he'll come and attack me and the mothers with¦15478 the children.
\v 12 But¦15504 you said,\x + \xo 32:12: \xt Gen 22:17.\x* ‘I will¦15504 surely cause things to prosper with¦15506 you, and¦15504 I'll make your descendants as \add numerous as\add* the sand \add grains on the beach\add* which are too many to be counted.’ ”
\p
\v 13 Then he stayed there for that¦15522 night and¦15511 he \add selected\add* gifts for his brother Esaw from¦15526 what he had¦15512 with¦15515 him:
\v 14 two hundred female and twenty male goats, two hundred ewes and twenty rams,
\v 15 thirty milk camels with their young, forty cows and ten bulls, and twenty¦15550 female¦15543 and ten male¦15545 donkeys.
\v 16 Then he \add handed them over\add* to his slaves, each herd by itself, and he \add ≈told them\add*, “Go ahead of¦15555 me \add one by one\add*, and \add keep a\add* space between each herd.”
\v 17 And he¦15568 instructed the¦15569 first one, saying, “When Esaw my¦15580 brother meets you and asks you, saying, ‘Who do you belong to, and where are you going? And who do those animals belong to?’
\v 18 then you¦15595 \add should answer\add*, ‘They belong¦15602 to your¦15609 servant Yacob. They are¦15606 a gift sent to my¦15598 master Esaw¦15597. \add In fact, he's coming along\add* behind us.’ ”
\v 19 Then \add Yacob\add* also¦15612 instructed the second and¦15612 third slaves, as well as everyone who followed behind¦15624 the herds, \add telling them\add*, “Say¦15612 the same thing to¦15613 Esaw¦15619 when you¦15612 find him,
\v 20 and also¦15627 say¦15644, ‘Look, your servant Yacob is behind¦15642 us.’ ” \add Yacob was thinking\add*, “I'll \add cheer him up\add* with¦15651 the¦15631 gifts that are going¦15641 ahead of¦15639 me, and after that, when¦15651 I see \add him in person\add*, perhaps he'll \add accept me\add*.”
\v 21 \add So\add* the gifts¦15666 went¦15667 ahead¦15668 of¦15658 him, and¦15669 he¦15675 himself stayed in¦15666 the camp for¦15661 that¦15667 night.
\s1 Yacob gets renamed after fighting at Penu'el
\rem /s1 Jacob Wrestles With God; Jacob Wrestles with God; Jacob Wrestles at Peniel
\p
\v 22 Then during that¦15688 night¦15685, \add Yacob\add* got up and took his two wives and \add their\add* two female slaves and his eleven sons and crossed over the ford of the Yabbok \add Stream\add*.
\v 23 \add After\add* crossing¦15710 the¦15699 stream, he¦15710 \add also\add* sent across \add everything else that\add* belonged to him.
\v 24 Then Yacob was left alone, and¦15716 a man wrestled with him¦15726 until dawn.\x + \xo 32:24-26: \xt Hos 12:3-4.\x*
\v 25 \add When the man realised\add* he wasn't gaining on him¦15735, he touched \add Yacob\add* on the socket of his hip so that it¦15732 became dislocated as \add they\add* wrestled¦15733 with¦15735 \add each other\add*.
\v 26 Then \add the man\add* said, “Release me \add now\add*, because¦15742 it's¦15750 \add already\add* dawning.”
\p “I won't¦15743 release you until you bless me,” Yacob¦15754 \add ≈responded\add*.
\p
\v 27 “What's your name?” the¦15763 man \add asked\add*.
\p “Yacob,” he¦15764 \add ≈replied¦15759\add*.
\p
\v 28 “You won't be called Yacob¦15779 anymore,” the man said¦15773, “but¦15773 \add you'll be\add* ‘Yisra'el’ \add !(‘Israel’,\add* \add which means ‘he struggled with God’)\add*, because you have struggled with God and with men, and you won.”\x + \xo 32:28: \xt Gen 35:10.\x*
\p
\v 29 “Please tell \add me\add* \em your¦15787\em* name¦15787,” Yacob¦15784 \add requested\add*.\x + \xo 32:29: \xt Jdg 13:17-18.\x*
\p “Why \add would you want to know\add* my name?” \add the man replied\add*, then he¦15782 blessed Yacob there.
\p
\v 30 \add So\add* Yacob¦15805 named the place ‘Penu'el’ \add (also spelt ‘Peni'el’, which means ‘God's face’)\add*, because he¦15804 said¦15806, “I saw God face to¦15812 face, yet my¦15815 life was preserved.”
\v 31 And¦15821 the¦15823 sun rose above him as he passed through Penu'el, and¦15834 he was limping because¦15826 of¦15823 his hip.
\v 32 That's why Israelis don't eat the muscle of the tendon that is on the hip socket to¦15840 this day, because \add the man\add* touched the socket of Yacob's hip on the muscle of the tendon.
\c 33
\s1 Esaw and Yacob meet peacefully
\rem /s1 Jacob and Esau Meet; Jacob Meets Esau; Jacob and Esau Make Peace
\p
\v 1 Then Yacob \add raised his head\add* and looked \add ahead\add*, and wow, Esaw¦15889 was¦15890 coming¦15890 \add towards him\add* along with¦15899 \add his\add* four¦15892 hundred¦15893 men¦15894. Then \add Yacob quickly\add* divided¦15895 the¦15898 children among Le'ah¦15901 and Rahel¦15904 and \add their\add* two¦15906 female slaves,
\v 2 and he¦15909 put the¦15912 slave women and their¦15915 children first¦15916, and then¦15909 Le'ah¦15919 and her¦15920 children after them, and \add finally\add* Rahel¦15924 and Yosef¦15927 after them.
\v 3 Then¦15930 he himself went¦15939 ahead¦15932 of them¦15932, and¦15930 he bowed¦15933 to¦15940 the ground seven¦15935 times¦15936 as he approached his¦15942 brother¦15942.
\v 4 \add But\add* Esaw¦15945 ran¦15944 \add forward\add* to¦15946 meet¦15946 him and embraced¦15947 him and \add threw his arms around\add* his neck¦15951 and kissed¦15953 him, and they¦15955 \add both\add* cried \add together\add*.
\v 5 Then¦15957 \add Esaw looked up\add* and saw¦15961 the women¦15964 and the children, and \add asked\add*, “Who¦15969 are¦15971 these¦15971 \add others\add* with you¦15972?”
\p “The¦15974 children that¦15969 God¦15978 has¦15977 graciously given to¦15977 your¦15981 servant¦15981,” Yacob replied¦15968.
\v 6 Then¦15983 the¦15984 \add two\add* female slaves approached¦15983 with their¦15986 children¦15986, and¦15983 they¦15985 bowed¦15987 down¦15987.
\v 7 \add Next\add* Le'ah¦15992 also¦15993 approached with her¦15993 children¦15993, and they bowed down. Then¦15989 after¦15995 that, Yosef¦15997 and Rahel¦15998 approached, and they bowed down.
\p
\v 8 Then¦16001 \add Esaw asked\add*, “What were all¦16004 these groups that¦16008 I¦16009 met¦16009?”
\p “To¦16011 \add win\add* my¦16015 master's¦16015 favour¦16013,” Yacob \add ≈replied¦16001\add*.
\p
\v 9 “I've got plenty, my¦16023 brother¦16023,” said¦16017 Esaw¦16018. “\add Keep\add* what belongs¦16024 to¦16021 you.”
\p
\v 10 “No¦16032, please¦16034,” Yacob¦16031 \add insisted\add*. “If¦16035 I've \add won\add* your¦16040 favour¦16039, then¦16030 take my¦16042 gifts¦16042. Because¦16044 indeed, I've seen¦16048 you face-to-face, which is like¦16050 seeing¦16050 God's¦16051 face, and¦16030 you've¦16053 received¦16053 me¦16053 \add favourably\add*.
\v 11 Please¦16057 take¦16055 my¦16060 \add gifts\add* that¦16061 were brought¦16062 to you, because¦16068 God¦16067 has¦16066 been gracious¦16066 to me¦16066 and¦16068 I have \add way more than I need\add*.”
\p And¦16074 he¦16074 urged¦16074 him¦16076, so \add Esaw\add* took¦16077 it¦16076
\v 12 and said¦16079, “\add Okay,\add* let's¦16080 get moving and go, and I'll¦16082 go ahead¦16083 of you¦16083.”
\p
\v 13 But¦16085 \add Yacob replied\add*, “My¦16087 master¦16087 knows¦16088 that¦16089 the children are¦16092 tender plus¦16093 I have flocks and¦16085 herds¦16094 \add with young animals\add*. If¦16089 they¦16097 drive¦16097 them¦16097 \add harder today\add*, then¦16085 all¦16101 the flocks will¦16092 die¦16100.
\v 14 Please¦16107 let¦16105 my master go on before¦16109 his¦16110 servant¦16110, and I'll¦16112 lead them on at my gentle¦16114 pace—at the pace of the livestock¦16116 that¦16117 are ahead of me and at the pace of the children—until¦16122 I come¦16125 to¦16126 my master's¦16128 \add place\add* in¦16109 Se'ir.”
\p
\v 15 “\add At least\add* let¦16133 me¦16133 leave some¦16137 of¦16137 my¦16149 men with¦16141 you¦16136 \add all\add*,” said¦16131 Esaw¦16132.
\p “Why do that¦16140?” \add contradicted Yacob\add*. “May my master¦16149 \add allow me to decide\add*.”
\v 16 \add So\add* Esaw¦16154 \add and his men\add* started heading \add home\add* to¦16156 Se'ir¦16156,
\v 17 \add but\add* Yacob travelled to¦16162 Succot \add where he\add* built¦16161 a house¦16163 for himself and¦16158 made¦16165 shelters for his¦16164 livestock¦16164. That's why he named the¦16171 place¦16173 ‘Succot’ \add (which means ‘shelters’)\add*.
\s1 Yacob begins to settle at Shekem
\rem /s1 Jacob Reaches Shechem
\p
\v 18 Then Yacob¦16178, having come from¦16186 Paddan¦16186-Aram, arrived peacefully at¦16185 the city of¦16180 Shekem¦16181 in¦16185 the Canaan¦16184 region¦16183, and¦16188 he¦16188 camped¦16188 \add outside\add* the city.
\v 19 \add In due course,\add* he bought¦16194 the portion¦16197 of the field¦16198 where¦16199 he'd pitched¦16200 his¦16203 tents¦16203 from¦16204 the sons¦16205 of Hamor¦16207, the father¦16208 of Shekem¦16209, for¦16210 a hundred¦16210 \add pieces of silver\add*.\x + \xo 33:19: \xt Josh 24:32; Yhn 4:5.\x*
\v 20 He¦16213 set¦16213 up¦16213 an¦16216 altar¦16216 there¦16215 and¦16213 he¦16213 named it¦16217 ‘El¦16220 Elohe¦16221 Yisra'el’ \add (which means ‘God, the god of Yisra'el’)\add*.
\c 34
\s1 Dinah's rape leads to a plan
\rem /s1 The Rape of Dinah; Dinah and the Shechemites; Revenge against Shechem
\p
\v 1 \add One day,\add* Yacob¦16232 and¦16225 Le'ah's¦16229 daughter¦16227 Dinah¦16226, went¦16225 out¦16225 to¦16232 \add visit some of\add* the¦16235 \add local women\add*,
\v 2 \add but\add* Shekem¦16239, the son¦16240 of Hamor¦16242 the Hivite¦16243, the ruler of the land¦16245, saw¦16237 her and he¦16248 \add grabbed\add* her and \add raped\add* her.
\v 3 \add Shekem admired Dinah so much that he fell in love with her\add* and¦16252 spoke¦16262 \add sweetly to her to try to cultivate a relationship\add*.
\v 4 Then¦16268 \add he asked\add* Hamor¦16272 his¦16273 father¦16273, “Get¦16269 this¦16281 girl¦16280 for¦16282 me¦16277 as a wife.”
\p
\v 5 \add When\add* Yacob heard¦16285 that¦16286 \add Shekem\add* had¦16287 dishonoured his daughter¦16291 Dinah¦16290, his sons¦16292 were¦16293 \add out\add* in¦16297 the¦16297 countryside¦16297 with¦16294 his livestock¦16296, so Yacob \add waited quietly\add* until¦16300 they¦16302 \add got home\add*.
\v 6 \add Meanwhile\add* Shekem's¦16308 father¦16306 Hamor¦16305 \add took Shekem\add* to¦16309 \add negotiate\add* with¦16313 Yacob¦16311.
\v 7 \add In due course\add* Yacob's sons¦16315 came¦16317 \add home\add* from¦16318 the¦16320 countryside¦16320. When¦16315 they heard¦16321 \add what had happened\add*, then¦16315 they¦16323 were¦16322 \add very angry and upset\add*, because¦16327 \add Shekem\add* had done¦16330 something disgraceful¦16329 to¦16325 Yisra'el¦16331 by¦16332 \add taking advantage of\add* Yacob's daughter¦16335. \add Something like that should never have happened\add*.
\v 8 \add But\add* Hamor¦16343 spoke¦16342 with them¦16344, saying¦16345, “My¦16347 son Shekem¦16346 my¦16347 son \add is madly in love with\add* your¦16350 daughter¦16350. Please¦16352 give¦16351 her¦16353 to¦16354 him¦16354 as a wife.
\v 9 \add In fact, why don't you allow us to marry\add* your daughters, and¦16357 you \add people could have\add* our¦16365 daughters \add to marry\add*,
\v 10 then¦16369 you could settle \add here permanently\add* with us. \add This region would open up to you all so\add* settle in¦16377 it¦16372 and trade¦16375 \add with us\add* and \add you could\add* buy \add more land around here\add*.”
\p
\v 11 Then¦16379 Shekem¦16380 spoke¦16379 to¦16381 \add Dinah's\add* father¦16383 and brothers¦16387, “\add Look kindly on my request\add* and I'll¦16395 give¦16395 \add whatever you ask for\add*.
\v 12 \add Set any very high amount for\add* the bride-price¦16400 and gifts¦16401 \add from\add* me, and I'll¦16402 give \add whatever you ask\add*—\add just\add* give the young woman¦16412 to¦16412 me as¦16403 a wife.”
\p
\v 13 \add But because\add* Shekem had¦16428 \add abused\add* their¦16431 sister¦16431 Dinah¦16430, Yacob's¦16417 sons¦16415 \add gave a false answer\add* to Shekem and¦16414 his¦16424 father¦16424,
\v 14 \add stating\add*, “\add Sorry,\add* we¦16436 can't¦16435 do¦16437 that¦16439 because¦16449 it¦16447 would be a disgrace \add in our culture\add* to allow a man¦16444 with a foreskin¦16448 \add to marry her\add*.
\v 15 We'd only¦16455 consent \add to your proposal\add* if¦16460 you'd become like¦16462 us¦16462 by¦16463 circumcising¦16463 every¦16465 male¦16467 among you,
\v 16 Then¦16469 we'd give¦16469 our¦16472 daughters to you \add to marry\add*, and we'd take¦16477 your daughters for¦16479 us¦16479 \add to marry\add*. And¦16480 we'd settle among you, and we \add all\add* would become¦16482 one¦16484 \add united\add* people¦16483.
\v 17 \add But\add* if¦16486 you¦16489 all won't¦16488 \add agree\add* to¦16490 get circumcised¦16491, then¦16486 we'll¦16492 \add just\add* take¦16492 \add Dinah\add* and¦16486 go¦16496.”
\p
\v 18 \add This idea\add* pleased¦16498 Hamor¦16501 and¦16502 his son¦16504,
\v 19 \add so Shekem\add* didn't¦16508 delay¦16510 \add implementing the plan\add* because¦16514 he was the most honoured¦16520 son in¦16516 his¦16523 father's¦16523 household¦16522, and¦16508 he was so delighted¦16515 with¦16516 Yacob's¦16518 daughter¦16516.
\p
\v 20 So¦16525 Hamor¦16526 and Shekem¦16527 went¦16525 to \add the city elders at\add* the gate¦16531 of¦16536 their city, and they¦16533 spoke¦16533 to them, saying¦16538,
\v 21 “These¦16541 men¦16540 are¦16542 peaceful¦16542 towards us¦16544, so let them settle in the land, and let them trade¦16547 in it¦16548. And¦16549 look, the land \add is plenty wide enough for them as well\add*. We¦16550 can take¦16558 their¦16557 daughters for ourselves as wives¦16561, and we¦16565 can give¦16565 our¦16564 daughters to them \add to marry\add*.
\v 22 \add But\add* they'll¦16571 only¦16568 consent to settle among us and unite with¦16570 us on¦16570 this¦16570 \add condition\add*: we would need to circumcise¦16579 all of¦16581 the¦16573 males¦16583 amongst us, just¦16584 like they're¦16585 circumcised¦16586.
\v 23 Won't all of¦16588 their livestock and their property¦16589 and all their animals¦16592 then belong to¦16594 us? \add So\add* let's¦16597 agree¦16597 with¦16600 \add their condition\add* and then they'll¦16599 settle among¦16600 us.”
\v 24 All¦16610 \add the elders\add* at the city gate \add agreed with\add* Hamor¦16605 and his¦16609 son¦16609 Shekem¦16608, and \add so\add* every¦16616 male¦16618 who¦16612 \add passed through\add* the city gate was circumcised¦16615.
\rem /s1 Dinah's Brothers Avenge Their Sister
\p
\v 25 Three days¦16626 later when \add all those men\add* were in¦16626 pain¦16629 \add from the cutting\add*, two¦16631 of¦16633 Yacob's¦16635 sons¦16633, Simeon¦16636 and Levi¦16637 who were Dinah's¦16639 brothers¦16638, took¦16630 their¦16628 swords¦16641 and boldly attacked the city¦16645 and slaughtered every¦16648 male¦16650,
\v 26 including¦16655 Hamor¦16654 and his¦16658 son¦16658 Shekem. Then¦16652 they¦16659 retrieved Dinah¦16666 from¦16667 Shekem's¦16657 house¦16667 and left \add the city\add*.
\v 27 Then when Yacob's¦16672 \add other sons\add* discovered that¦16679 a slaughter \add had taken place\add*, they¦16673 looted the¦16676 city¦16678 because their¦16681 sister¦16681 had been violated—
\v 28 taking¦16701 flocks¦16685 and herds¦16688 and donkeys¦16691—anything the was¦16696 in the city¦16696 or \add out\add* in the countryside¦16700.
\v 29 They¦16716 captured their women and children and took¦16716 all¦16710 their wealth¦16707 and everything that¦16721 was¦16722 in¦16722 their houses¦16722.
\p
\v 30 \add Afterwards,\add* Yacob¦16725 scolded Simeon¦16728 and Levi¦16731, saying¦16724, “You \add two\add* have¦16732 \add made\add* trouble¦16732 for me by¦16734 \add destroying my reputation with\add* those who live in¦16735 the land—the Canaanites¦16737 and the Perizzites¦16738. \add There's only a few of us\add*, and if they¦16742 gather¦16742 together to¦16726 attack¦16744 me \add and my household\add*, then¦16724 \add we'll\add* be¦16745 destroyed¦16745—me and \add all\add* my household.”
\p
\v 31 \add But\add* they¦16749 \add ≈responded¦16749\add*, “Should he¦16751 \add have gotten away with\add* treating our¦16754 sister¦16754 like¦16750 a prostitute¦16750?”
\c 35
\s1 God blesses Yacob at Beyt-El
\rem /s1 Jacob Returns to Bethel; Jacob's Return to Bethel; God Blesses Jacob at Bethel
\p
\v 1 Then God¦16758 told Yacob¦16761, “\add Pack\add* up¦16763 and \add move\add* to Beyt¦16764-El and settle¦16767 there, and make¦16770 an¦16773 altar¦16773 there to the god¦16774 who¦16775 appeared¦16775 to you when¦16777 you were fleeing¦16777 from¦16779 your brother¦16781 Esaw¦16780.”\x + \xo 35:1: \xt Gen 28:11-17.\x*
\p
\v 2 \add So\add* Yacob¦16784 instructed his¦16787 household¦16787 and \add everyone\add* with¦16799 him¦16792, “Remove¦16794 \add any\add* foreign¦16797 \add idols\add* that¦16798 are¦16799 among¦16799 you¦16799, and purify¦16800 yourselves¦16800 and change¦16801 your¦16802 clothes¦16802.
\v 3 Then¦16804 \add we'll leave here\add* and \add make the uphill trip\add* to Beyt¦16806-El, and I'll¦16809 make an¦16812 altar¦16812 there¦16811 to God¦16813, who¦16814 answered¦16814 me \add when I was\add* distressed¦16817. He's¦16818 been with¦16820 me \add wherever\add* I've¦16822 gone¦16822.”
\v 4 So¦16824 they¦16824 gave¦16824 Yacob¦16827 all¦16829 \add their idols and their earrings\add*, and \add he buried\add* them¦16841 under¦16843 the¦16844 terebinth¦16844 tree that¦16833 was¦16846 near¦16846 Shekem¦16848.
\p
\v 5 Then¦16850 they \add started their journey but\add* and¦16850 the \add people of\add* the cities¦16857 around¦16859 them \add were afraid of Yacob's god\add* so they didn't¦16860 \add attack them\add*.
\v 6 \add So\add* Yacob¦16867 and¦16866 everyone¦16879 with¦16882 him¦16882 \add eventually arrived\add* at Luz¦16868 in¦16870 the¦16879 Canaan¦16871 region¦16870 (which¦16880 is¦16873 \add now named\add* Beyt¦16873-El).
\v 7 He¦16884 built¦16884 an¦16886 altar¦16886 there and¦16884 named the place¦16888 ‘El Beyt-El’ \add (which means ‘the god of Beyt-El’)\add*, because¦16893 God had¦16895 revealed¦16895 himself to¦16896 him¦16896 there when¦16898 he¦16898 was fleeing¦16898 from¦16899 his¦16900 brother¦16900 \add Esaw\add*.
\v 8 Then¦16902 Rebekah's¦16905 nurse¦16904 Deborah¦16903 died¦16902 and¦16902 was¦16906 buried¦16906 under¦16907 the oak tree below Beyt¦16908-El \add so\add* he¦16913 named \add the place\add* ‘Allon Bakut’ \add (which means ‘oak of weeping’)\add*.
\p
\v 9 \add Now that Yacob had left\add* Paddan¦16926-Aram and \add come back to Beyt-El\add*, God¦16920 appeared¦16919 to him \add there\add* again¦16924 and blessed¦16928 him
\v 10 and¦16931 told him, “Your¦16940 name¦16935 is¦16936 Yacob \add but\add* you won't¦16937 be called Yacob anymore. \add From now on,\add* you'll be called Yisra'el (‘Israel¦16947’).” So¦16931 \add again\add*\x + \xo 35:10: \xt Gen 32:28.\x* \add God\add* called \add him\add* Yisra'el.
\v 11 Then¦16956 God \add also told\add* him¦16957, “I'm¦16959 God the provider. Be¦16962 fruitful¦16962 and multiply¦16963. Out¦16971 of you¦16968 will come a nation¦16964 and a community of nations¦16966, and kings¦16969 will come from¦16968 your¦16970 \add descendants\add*,\x + \xo 35:11-12: \xt Gen 17:4-8.\x*
\v 12 and I'll give you the land that¦16976 I gave¦16977 to Abraham¦16978 and to Isaac¦16979, and \add in the future,\add* I'll give the land to your¦16982 descendants¦16982 after¦16983 you.”
\v 13 After God¦16991 finished talking to Yacob, he¦16995 left him¦16990 and¦16989 went \add back\add* up.
\v 14 Then¦16998 Yacob¦16999 set¦16998 up¦16998 a stone¦17007 pillar¦17000 at¦17001 the place¦17001 where¦17002 \add God\add* had¦17004 spoken¦17004 with¦17001 him¦17005, and¦16998 he¦17004 poured¦17008 a drink¦17010 offering¦17010 over it¦17005, followed by oil¦17013,\x + \xo 35:14-15: \xt Gen 28:18-19.\x*
\v 15 and¦17015 he¦17015 named that place¦17020 ‘Beyt¦17026-El’ \add (which means ‘God's house’) because\add* God had¦17022 spoken¦17022 to him¦17023 there¦17024.
\s1 Rahel dies giving birth to Benyamin
\rem /s1 The Birth of Benjamin and the Death of Rachel; The Death of Rachel; The Deaths of Rachel and Isaac
\p
\v 16 Then they¦17030 moved on¦17044 from¦17031 Beyt¦17031-El, and¦17030 there was still quite a distance¦17036 to go¦17039 to reach to \add the town of\add* Efrath¦17040. Then Rahel¦17042 \add went into labour\add*, but¦17030 was having¦17043 great difficulty¦17043 in¦17044 \add delivering the baby\add*.
\v 17 \add At one point\add* when¦17047 she¦17047 was¦17046 in¦17047 hard labour¦17048, the¦17051 midwife¦17051 said¦17049 to¦17060 her¦17048, “Don't¦17052 be¦17054 afraid¦17054. You've got another son¦17061.”
\v 18 \add But Rahel\add* was dying¦17067, and¦17063 as her \add spirit\add* was leaving her, she¦17067 named¦17068 \add the baby\add* ‘Ben'oni’ \add (which means ‘son of my sorrow’)\add*, but¦17063 \add afterwards Yacob\add* renamed him¦17076 ‘Benyamin¦17077’ \add (which means ‘son of my right hand’)\add*.
\p
\v 19 \add Thus\add* Rahel¦17080 died¦17079 and¦17081 was¦17081 buried¦17081 on¦17082 the¦17082 path¦17082 to Efrath¦17083 (which¦17084 is¦17085 \add now called\add* Bethlehem).
\v 20 Yacob¦17089 set¦17088 up¦17088 a \add stone\add* pillar over¦17091 her¦17093 grave—that's the¦17095 pillar of¦17095 Rahel's¦17098 grave to this day¦17101.
\v 21 Then¦17103 Yisra'el¦17104 \add and his household moved on\add* and pitched¦17105 \add their\add* tents¦17106 beyond¦17108 Migdal Eder¦17111.
\s1 Yisra'el's sons
\rem /s1 The Sons of Jacob
\r (1 Chr. 2:1-2)
\p
\v 22 While they¦17133 were¦17133 living¦17114 in¦17114 that¦17117 area, \add Yisra'el's son\add* Reuben¦17119 slept with¦17114 his¦17127 father's¦17127 slave-wife Bilhah¦17124, and¦17113 Yisra'el found out \add about it\add*.\x + \xo 35:22: \xt Gen 49:4.\x*
\p \add Yisra'el\add* now had twelve sons¦17134.
\v 23 Le'ah's¦17141 sons¦17140 were his eldest son¦17140 Reuven¦17144, and then to Simeon¦17145, Levi¦17146, Yihudah¦17147, Yissakar¦17148, and Zebulun¦17149.
\v 24 Rahel's¦17152 sons¦17151 were Yosef¦17153 and¦17154 Benyamin¦17154.
\v 25 The¦17156 sons¦17156 of¦17157 Rahel's¦17159 slave woman Bilhah¦17157 were Dan¦17160 and¦17161 Naftali¦17161.
\v 26 The¦17163 sons of¦17164 Leah's¦17166 slave women Zilpah¦17164 were Gad¦17167 and¦17163 Asher¦17168. Those were \add Yisra'el's\add* sons who¦17172 were \add nearly all\add* born¦17173 in¦17176 Paddan¦17176-Aram.
\s1 Yitshak's death
\rem /s1 The Death of Isaac
\p
\v 27 Then¦17179 \add Yisra'el\add* went¦17179 VISIT OR LIVE? to¦17181 his¦17184 father¦17184 Yitshak in Mamre¦17185 (\add also named\add* Kiriat¦17186-Arba or Hebron¦17189), where¦17190 Abraham¦17195 and¦17196 Yitshak had¦17192 \add lived for a time\add*.\x + \xo 35:27: \xt Gen 13:18.\x*
\v 28 Yitshak¦17200 lived to be 180 years¦17202 old.
\v 29 Then¦17206 Yitshak¦17207, having lived a long and full life, \add breathed his last\add* and died¦17208 and \add joined his ancestors\add*, and his sons¦17220 Esaw¦17218 and \add Yisra'el\add* buried¦17216 him¦17217.
\c 36
\s1 Esaw's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Esau; Descendants of Esau; Esau's Descendants
\r (1 Chr. 1:34-37)
\p
\v 1 These¦17223 are¦17224 the¦17224 descendants of¦17224 Esaw¦17225 (who's also¦17223 known as Edom¦17227):
\v 2 Esaw¦17229 had \add married two local women from the Canaan region\add*: Adah¦17238 (the Hittite¦17242 Elon's¦17241 daughter) and Oholibamah¦17245 (Anah's¦17248 daughter and granddaughter of¦17246 the Hivite¦17252 Zibeon¦17251)\x + \xo 36:2: \xt Gen 26:34.\x*
\v 3 and Basemat¦17256 (Yishma'el's¦17259 daughter¦17257 and the¦17257 sister¦17260 of¦17257 Nevayot¦17261).\x + \xo 36:3: \xt Gen 28:9.\x*
\p
\v 4 Esaw's¦17265 \add wife\add* Adah¦17264 gave birth to Elifaz¦17268, and¦17263 Basemat¦17269 gave birth to Reuel¦17273.
\v 5 Oholibamah gave birth to¦17293 Yeush¦17279 and Yalam¦17283 and Korah¦17286. Those were Esaw's¦17289 sons¦17288 who¦17290 were born¦17291 in¦17294 the¦17288 Canaan¦17295 region¦17294.
\p
\v 6 Then¦17297 Esaw¦17298 took¦17297 his wives¦17301 and his sons¦17304 and daughters¦17307 and all his household¦17313, and his livestock¦17316 and all his animals¦17321 and all his possessions that¦17326 he had¦17327 acquired¦17325 in¦17328 the¦17312 Canaan¦17329 region¦17328, and he went¦17330 to¦17331 a place \add far away\add* from¦17334 his brother¦17336 Yisra'el¦17335,
\v 7 because they had too¦17342 many possessions for¦17343 them to remain together¦17344, and¦17345 the¦17347 land¦17347 they were staying¦17343 in¦17351 wasn't able¦17346 to support¦17349 \add both of\add* them because of \add all\add* their¦17352 livestock¦17352.
\v 8 \add So\add* Esaw¦17355 (\add also known\add* as Edom¦17360) settled \add his household\add* in¦17356 the¦17356 Se'ir hills.
\p
\v 9 Now these¦17362 are¦17363 the \add following\add* generations of Esaw¦17364, the father of the Edomites in¦17367 the Se'ir hills:
\v 10 \add Two of\add* Esaw's¦17374 sons¦17372 were Elifaz¦17375, who he had with his wife Adah¦17378, and Reuel¦17381, who he had with his wife Basemat¦17384.
\p
\v 11 Elifaz's¦17390 sons¦17389 were¦17388 Teman¦17391, Omar¦17392, Zefo¦17393 and Gatam¦17394 and Kenaz¦17395.
\v 12 Elifaz and his slave-wife Timna had \add a son they named\add* Amalek¦17409. (Elifaz's¦17401 sons¦17411 \add were all\add* grandsons of¦17411 Esaw¦17404 and his wife Adah¦17412.)
\v 13 Reuel's¦17418 sons¦17417 were Nahat¦17419, Zerah¦17420, Shammah¦17421, and Mizzah¦17422. (They¦17425 were the¦17426 grandsons of¦17426 Esaw¦17429 and his wife¦17428 Basemat¦17427.)
\v 14 Esaw¦17442 and his wife¦17441 Oholibamah had Yeush¦17447, Yalam¦17451, and Korah¦17454. (\add Oholibamah was\add* the daughter¦17435 of Anah¦17437 and the granddaughter of Zibeon¦17440.)
\rem /s1 Clans and Kings of Edom
\p
\v 15 These¦17456 were Esaw's sons \add who became\add* leaders: the¦17461 sons of¦17461 Elifaz¦17462 (Esaw's eldest son) \add which were\add* Chief Teman¦17466, Chief Omar¦17468, Chief Zefo¦17470, Chief Kenaz¦17472,
\v 16 Chief Korah¦17476, Chief Gatam¦17478, Chief Amalek¦17480. Those were the chiefs¦17482 of Elifaz¦17483 in¦17484 the Edom¦17485 region¦17484. (They were the grandsons of \add Esaw and his wife\add* Adah¦17488.)
\p
\v 17 These¦17490 were the sons¦17491 of Esaw's¦17495 son¦17493 Reuel: Chief Nahath¦17497, Chief Zerah¦17499, Chief Shammah¦17501, Chief Mizzah¦17503. Those were the chiefs¦17505 of Reuel in¦17507 the Edom¦17508 region¦17507. (They were the grandsons of Esaw and¦17490 his wife¦17512 Basemath¦17511.)
\p
\v 18 These¦17515 were the sons¦17516 of¦17516 Esaw's wife Oholibamah: Chief Yeush¦17521, Chief Yalam¦17523, Chief Korah¦17525. (They were the chiefs¦17527 from Esaw's wife Oholibamah—Anah's¦17531 daughter¦17529.)
\v 19 Those were the¦17536 sons¦17536 of¦17536 Esaw¦17538 (\add also known\add* as Edom¦17542), and¦17539 those¦17539 were their¦17540 chiefs¦17540.
\s1 Se'ir's descendants
\rem /s1 The Descendants of Seir; Original Peoples of Edom
\r (1 Chr. 1:38-42)
\p
\v 20 These¦17545 were the sons¦17546 of¦17546 Se'ir¦17548 the Horite¦17549 who were inhabiting¦17550 \add that area\add*: Lotan¦17552, Shobal¦17553, Zibeon¦17554, Anah¦17555,
\v 21 Dishon¦17557, Ezer¦17558, and¦17557 Dishan¦17559. Those were¦17561 the chiefs¦17561 of¦17561 the Horites¦17562, Se'ir's sons¦17563, in¦17565 the Edom¦17566 region¦17565.
\v 22 Lotan's¦17571 sons¦17569 were¦17568 Hori¦17572 and Hemam¦17573, and his sister¦17574 was¦17574 Timna¦17576.
\v 23 Shobal's¦17580 sons¦17579 were¦17579 Alvan¦17581, Manahath¦17582, Ebal¦17583, Shefo¦17584, and¦17578 Onam¦17585.
\v 24 Zibeon's¦17590 sons¦17588 were¦17588 Aiah¦17591 and¦17587 Anah. This is the same Anah who¦17595 discovered some hot¦17599 springs¦17599 while he¦17593 was¦17601 out in¦17600 the desert grazing his¦17606 father's¦17606 donkeys¦17604.
\v 25 Anah's¦17611 children¦17609 were¦17609 \add his son\add* Dishon¦17612 and¦17613 his daughter¦17614 Oholibamah¦17613.
\v 26 Dishon's¦17620 sons¦17619 were¦17619 Hemdan¦17621, Eshban¦17622, Ithran¦17623, and¦17618 Keran¦17624.
\v 27 Ezer's¦17629 sons¦17627 were¦17627 Bilhan¦17630, Zaavan¦17631, and¦17631 Akan¦17632.
\v 28 Dishan's¦17637 sons¦17635 were¦17635 Uz¦17638 and¦17639 Aran¦17639.
\v 29 These¦17641 were¦17642 the chiefs¦17642 of¦17642 the Horites¦17643: Chief Lotan¦17645, Chief Shobal¦17647, Chief Zibeon¦17649, Chief Anah¦17651,
\rem /s1 The Kings of Edom
\rem /r (1 Chronicles 1.43-54)
\v 30 Chief Dishon¦17654, Chief Ezer¦17656, Chief Dishan¦17658. Those were¦17660 the chiefs¦17660 of¦17660 the Horites¦17661, according¦17662 to¦17662 their¦17662 clans in¦17663 the Se'ir region¦17663.
\s1 The kings around Edom
\rem /s1 The Rulers of Edom; Rulers of Edom
\r (1 Chr. 1:43-54)
\p
\v 31 And¦17667 these¦17667 were¦17668 the kings¦17668 who¦17669 reigned¦17674 in¦17671 Edom¦17672 region¦17671 before¦17673 the tribes of¦17678 Yisra'el¦17678 had a king¦17676:
\v 32 Beor's¦17685 son¦17683 Bela¦17682 reigned¦17680 in¦17681 Edom¦17681, and¦17686 the¦17683 name¦17686 of¦17683 his city¦17687 was¦17688 Dinhabah¦17688.
\v 33 Then¦17690 Bela¦17691 died¦17690, and¦17692 Zerah's¦17697 son¦17695 Yobab¦17694 from¦17698 Bozrah¦17698 reigned¦17692 in¦17693 his¦17693 place¦17693.
\v 34 Then¦17700 Yobab¦17701 died¦17700, and¦17700 Husham¦17704 from¦17705 the land¦17705 of¦17705 the Temanites¦17706 reigned¦17702 in¦17703 his¦17703 place¦17703.
\v 35 Then¦17708 Husham¦17709 died¦17708, and Bedad's¦17715 son¦17713 Hadad¦17712 reigned¦17710 in his place¦17711, and the name¦17722 of¦17713 his city¦17723 was Avith¦17724. \add He was the one\add* who¦17716 attacked the Midianites in the countryside¦17720 in Moab¦17721.
