\p
\v 40 And¦17773 these¦17773 are the names of the chiefs¦17775 of Esaw¦17776, according to their clans¦17777, according to their places¦17778, by¦17779 their names: Chief Timna¦17781, Chief Alvan, Chief Yetet¦17785,
\v 41 Chief Oholibamah¦17788, Chief Elah¦17790, Chief Pinon¦17792,
\v 42 Chief Kenaz¦17795, Chief Teman¦17797, Chief Mibzar¦17799,
\v 43 Chief Magdiel¦17802, and Chief Iram¦17804. They were¦17807 the chiefs¦17803 of Edom¦17808, according¦17809 to¦17809 their dwellings¦17809 in¦17810 the land¦17810 of their possession¦17811. That¦17812 was¦17813 \add the record about\add* Esaw¦17813, the ancestor¦17814 of the Edomites.
\c 37
\s1 Yosef's dreams cause conflict
\rem /s1 Joseph; Joseph Dreams of Greatness; Joseph's Dreams; Joseph and His Brothers
\p
\v 1 Yisra'el¦17819 \add !(Israel/Yacob¦17819/Jacob)\add* lived¦17818 in the area where his¦17822 father¦17822 \add Yitshak\add* had stayed, in the Canaan¦17824 region¦17820.
\v 2 This is \add the record of his\add* descendants:
\p When¦17830 Yosef \add !(Joseph¦17830)\add* was seventeen¦17833, he¦17837 was shepherding¦17838 the flocks¦17842 with¦17842 his brothers. He¦17843 was \add younger than his brothers\add* who were the sons¦17831 of his father's¦17854 \add slave\add* wives¦17853 Bilhah¦17848 and¦17855 Zilpah¦17852. \add One day\add* Yosef gave a \add tell-tale\add* bad¦17860 report¦17859 about¦17828 them to¦17861 their¦17863 father.
\p
\v 3 \add Now\add* Yisra'el loved¦17866 Yosef¦17869 more than all¦17870 of¦17872 his¦17872 \add other\add* sons¦17872 because¦17873 he was \add born to him when he was already old\add*, so he made¦17880 a multicoloured robe for him.
\v 4 \add When\add* his brothers saw¦17885 that¦17887 their¦17891 father¦17891 loved¦17890 \add Yosef\add* more than all¦17892 of¦17894 his brothers, they¦17895 hated¦17895 him and¦17885 couldn't \add make themselves say anything nice\add* to¦17900 him.
\p
\v 5 \add One night\add* Yosef¦17903 had a dream¦17904, and¦17902 he¦17902 told¦17905 his¦17906 brothers¦17906 about¦17906 it¦17910, \add but\add* that made them hate¦17909 him¦17910 even more.
\v 6 He¦17912 \add ≈told\add* them¦17913, “Please¦17916 \add listen about\add* this¦17918 dream¦17917 that¦17919 I¦17920 \add had\add*:
\v 7 See, we¦17923 were¦17924 \add tying up bundles of grain stalks\add* in¦17926 the¦17926 middle¦17926 of a field¦17927, and wow, my bundle got up and stood¦17933 upright¦17933. Then¦17922 \add would you believe it\add*, your bundles gathered¦17935 around and bowed¦17937 down¦17937 to¦17938 my \add one\add*.”
\p
\v 8 “Will¦17944 you really reign¦17944 over us?” his brothers¦17942 \add retorted\add*. “\add Do you think\add* you'll really rule¦17948 over us?” \add So\add* they¦17951 continued¦17940 to¦17953 hate¦17953 him¦17954 even more because of his dreams¦17957 and¦17940 because of \add what he said\add*.
\p
\v 9 Then¦17962 \add Yosef had\add* another dream, and \add again\add* he¦17966 told his¦17968 brothers¦17968, “Listen, I¦17971 \add had\add* another dream, and wow, the sun¦17975 and the moon¦17976 and eleven stars¦17979 were¦17980 bowing¦17980 down¦17980 to¦17981 me¦17981.”
\p
\v 10 \add When\add* he¦17983 told his father and his brothers, his father scolded him¦17995, “What's¦17996 this¦17998 dream¦17997 that¦17999 you \add had\add*? Will¦18002 your mother¦18004 and I¦18003 and your brothers \add actually\add* come¦18001 to bow¦18006 down¦18006 to the¦18008 ground¦18008 to you?”
\v 11 \add Consequently\add* his brothers¦18013 envied him¦18012, but¦18010 his father¦18014 \add kept pondering it\add*.\x + \xo 37:11: \xt Acts 7:9.\x*
\s1 Yosef is sold by his brothers
\rem /s1 Joseph Is Sold and Taken to Egypt; Joseph Is Sold by His Brothers; Joseph Sold by His Brothers
\p
\v 12 \add Sometime after that, Yosef's\add* brothers¦18021 went¦18020 to¦18022 shepherd their¦18027 father's¦18027 flocks¦18025 near Shekem¦18028.
\v 13 \add One day,\add* Yisra'el¦18031 said¦18030 to Yosef¦18034, “Aren't your¦18036 brothers¦18036 \add away\add* shepherding near Shekem¦18038? Come¦18039 \add we'll get some supplies\add* and¦18030 I'll¦18040 send¦18040 you¦18040 to them¦18041.”
\p “\add Sure\add*,” Yosef \add ≈replied¦18030\add*.
\p
\v 14 Then¦18046 he¦18046 \add ≈told\add* him¦18062, “Please¦18050 go¦18048 and see¦18051 \add how\add* your¦18055 brothers¦18055 \add are doing\add* and the¦18063 flocks¦18059 \add as well\add*, then¦18046 \add come and let me know\add*.”
\p So¦18046 he sent¦18062 \add Yosef off\add* from¦18063 the Hebron¦18064 valley¦18063, and¦18060 he went¦18065 to¦18066 Shekem¦18066.
\v 15 \add When he got there,\add* a man¦18069 \add noticed\add* him wandering¦18071 around the¦18074 countryside¦18072 and¦18068 asked¦18073 him, “What¦18070 are¦18078 you¦18078 looking¦18078 for¦18078?”
\p
\v 16 “I'm¦18084 looking for¦18085 my¦18083 brothers¦18083,” he¦18080 answered¦18080. “Please¦18088 tell¦18086 me¦18089 where¦18090 they're \add looking after our flocks\add*.”
\p
\v 17 “They've¦18108 left¦18096 here¦18097,” the¦18095 man¦18095 responded¦18094. “I¦18099 heard¦18099 them¦18099 say, ‘Let's¦18101 go¦18101 to¦18102 Dothan¦18102.’ ”
\rem /s1 Joseph Sold into Slavery
\p So¦18094 Yosef¦18104 continued¦18094 on¦18108 after¦18105 his¦18106 brothers¦18106 and¦18094 found¦18107 them at¦18108 Dotan¦18102,
\v 18 \add but\add* they saw¦18110 him from¦18112 a distance¦18112 and¦18110 before¦18113 he¦18114 \add got close\add* to them¦18115, they \add made plans among themselves\add* to kill¦18118 him.
\v 19 “Look¦18125, that¦18128 master¦18126 \add dreamer\add* is¦18129 coming¦18129,” they¦18120 said¦18120 to¦18122 each¦18121 other.
\v 20 “\add Here's our chance to\add* kill¦18134 him and¦18131 throw¦18135 \add his body\add* into one¦18136 of¦18136 the¦18137 pits¦18137 \add around here\add*. We'll say¦18138 that a \add wild\add* animal¦18139 \add attacked\add* him. \em Then¦18131\em* we'll¦18138 see¦18142 what¦18143 his dreams¦18146 \add turn into\add*.”
\p
\v 21 But¦18148 Reuben¦18149 heard \add what they were saying\add*, and¦18148 he¦18150 \add tried to\add* rescue¦18150 \add Yosef by insisting\add*, “Let's¦18154 not¦18153 \add take\add* his life¦18155.”
\v 22 “Don't¦18161 shed¦18163 blood¦18165,” he continued¦18157. “Throw¦18166 him into¦18168 \add that\add* pit¦18170 in¦18173 the¦18170 wilderness¦18173, but¦18157 don't¦18161 \add wound\add* him.” \add He said this\add* because he was planning to rescue¦18181 Yosef \add later\add* and¦18157 return him \add home\add* to \add their\add* father¦18187.
\v 23 \add So\add* when Yosef¦18193 \add reached\add* his¦18196 brothers¦18196, they¦18197 \add grabbed him and ripped\add* off the¦18206 coloured¦18207 robe¦18203 that¦18208 \add he was wearing\add*.
\v 24 Then¦18211 they¦18211 took¦18211 him and¦18211 \add dropped\add* him into the pit. (The¦18213 pit was¦18216 empty¦18216 with no water¦18219 in¦18218 \add the bottom\add*).
\p
\v 25 Then¦18221 they sat down to eat¦18222, and \add a bit later when they looked up\add*, look, \add there was\add* a caravan¦18229 of¦18226 Yishma'elites¦18230 coming¦18231 \add towards them\add*. \add They had left\add* Gilead¦18232 carrying¦18239 spices¦18235 and balm¦18236 and myrrh¦18237 on their¦18226 camels¦18233 that they were¦18238 going to take¦18239 down to Egypt¦18240.
\v 26 Then¦18242 Yehudah¦18243 \add !(Judah¦18243)\add* said¦18242 to¦18244 his brothers¦18246, “What¦18247 will¦18251 we gain if we kill¦18251 our¦18254 brother¦18254 and¦18242 cover¦18255 up his blood¦18258?
\v 27 Let's¦18261 sell¦18261 him to¦18262 \add these\add* Ishmaelites¦18262 \add so we won't be guilty of killing\add* him. \add After all,\add* he's¦18273 our brother¦18271—our \add own\add* flesh¦18272 \add and blood\add*.” And¦18261 his¦18275 brothers¦18275 \add agreed\add*.
\v 28 \add So when\add* the Midianite traders came past, \add the brothers\add* pulled Yosef up¦18282 and lifted¦18282 him out of the pit¦18288 and sold¦18289 \add him\add* to the Ishmaelites¦18293 for¦18294 twenty¦18294 \add pieces\add* of silver¦18295. Then¦18277 \add the traders took\add* Yosef \add onwards with them\add* to Mitsrayim¦18300.\x + \xo 37:28: \xt Acts 7:9.\x*
\p
\v 29 \add Later on,\add* Reuben¦18303 returned¦18302 to¦18304 the¦18306 pit¦18306, but¦18302 wow, Yosef¦18310 wasn't \add down there\add*, so he¦18312 tore¦18312 his¦18315 clothes¦18315 \add in despair\add*
\v 30 and returned¦18317 to his¦18320 brothers¦18320 and said¦18321, “The¦18322 boy¦18322 isn't¦18323 there. \add What am I going to do\add*?”
\p
\v 31 Then¦18330 they¦18330 slaughtered¦18335 a young goat¦18336 and took¦18330 Yosef's¦18334 robe¦18341 and dipped¦18338 \add it\add* in¦18342 the¦18341 blood¦18342.
\v 32 Then¦18344 they¦18344 sent¦18344 the¦18347 coloured¦18348 robe \add back\add* to¦18350 their¦18352 father¦18352 with this message: “We¦18355 found¦18355 this. Please¦18358 examine whether or¦18362 not¦18364 it's¦18361 your son's¦18360 robe.”
\p
\v 33 \add Yisra'el\add* recognised¦18366 it¦18366 and¦18366 said¦18367, “It¦18367 \em is\em* my son's¦18369 robe. A \add wild\add* animal¦18370 \add must have eaten\add* him¦18372. Yosef¦18375 was \add almost\add* certainly torn¦18374 to¦18374 pieces¦18374.”
\v 34 Then¦18377 Yacob¦18378 ripped his clothes¦18379 and put sackcloth¦18381 \add around\add* his \add waist\add*, and he¦18383 mourned¦18383 for his son¦18386 many¦18388 days¦18387.
\v 35 All¦18391 his \add other\add* sons¦18393 and all¦18394 his daughters¦18396 \add came\add* to comfort¦18397 him, but¦18390 he¦18398 refused¦18398 to be¦18399 comforted¦18399 and said¦18400, “I¦18403 will¦18403 go¦18403 down¦18403 to my¦18406 son¦18406 in \add the grave\add* mourning¦18407.” So¦18390 his father¦18411 wept¦18409 for¦18409 him.
\p
\v 36 Meanwhile, the Midianites \add arrived\add* in Egypt¦18418 and¦18413 sold¦18414 him¦18415 to¦18419 Potifar¦18419—Far'oh's¦18421 captain of¦18420 the guards¦18423.
\c 38
\s1 Yehudah mistreats Tamar
\rem /s1 Judah and Tamar
\p
\v 1 \add Around that time\add*, Yehudah¦18430 \add !(Judah¦18430)\add* went down \add the hills\add* from¦18431 \add where\add* his¦18432 brothers¦18432 \add were\add* and¦18433 settled¦18433 near an¦18437 Adullamite¦18437 man¦18436 named Hirah¦18439.
\v 2 There¦18443 Yehudah¦18444 saw¦18441 the daughter¦18445 of¦18445 a Canaanite¦18448 man¦18447 named Shua¦18450, and¦18441 he¦18451 took¦18451 her \add as a wife\add*. He¦18452 \add slept with\add* her
\v 3 and she¦18456 got pregnant¦18455 and \add in due course\add*, gave¦18456 birth¦18456 to a son¦18457 who Yehudah named Er¦18462.
\v 4 Then¦18464 she got pregnant¦18464 again¦18465 and gave¦18466 birth¦18466 a \add another\add* son¦18467, and she named¦18468 him Onan¦18469.
\v 5 Then¦18474 she \add got pregnant yet again\add* and gave¦18476 birth¦18476 to a \add third\add* son¦18477, and she named¦18478 him¦18486 Shelah¦18482. (\add Yehudah\add* was in¦18484 Kezib¦18484 when¦18485 she \add Onan was born\add*.)
\p
\v 6 \add In due course,\add* Yehudah¦18489 took¦18488 a wife¦18490 for¦18491 Er¦18491 his¦18492 firstborn¦18492, and¦18493 her¦18493 name¦18493 was¦18494 Tamar¦18494.
\v 7 But¦18496 Er¦18497 was¦18496 evil¦18500 in¦18501 Yahweh's¦18502 eyes¦18501, so Yahweh killed him¦18503.
\v 8 Then¦18506 Yehudah¦18507 said¦18506 to Onan¦18508, “Go¦18509 to your brother's¦18513 wife¦18512 and¦18506 perform¦18514 your \add brotherly\add* duty to her¦18515 \add to\add* produce \add children\add* for¦18518 your brother.”
\v 9 However, Onan¦18521 knew¦18520 that \add any children\add* wouldn't be his, so \add whenever\add* he \add slept with\add* his brother's¦18535 wife¦18534, he spilled¦18536 \add his semen\add* on the¦18527 ground so that he wouldn't give¦18539 \add children\add* to¦18538 his \add late\add* brother.
\v 10 \add But\add* Yahweh¦18546 \add considered\add* what he did¦18548 to¦18549 be evil¦18544, so he killed him¦18552 also¦18549.
\v 11 Then¦18554 Yehudah¦18555 \add ≈told\add* his daughter-in-law¦18557 Tamar, “Remain a widow¦18559 in your father's¦18562 house until¦18563 my¦18567 son¦18567 Shelah¦18566 grows¦18565 up¦18565.” \add This was\add* because¦18568 he'd said¦18569, “So that he doesn't also¦18573 die¦18572 like¦18576 his brothers¦18576!” So Tamar went¦18577 and¦18554 lived in the¦18580 house of¦18580 her¦18581 father.
\p
\v 12 \add A long time afterwards\add*, Yehudah's¦18591 wife¦18589 (Shua's¦18588 daughter¦18586) died¦18585. \add After\add* Yehudah \add had finished mourning\add*, he¦18592 and¦18583 his friend Hirah¦18600 (the Adullamite¦18602) went to Timnah¦18603 to \add where\add* the men were shearing¦18597 his sheep¦18598.
\v 13 Then¦18605 Tamar¦18606 was¦18605 told¦18605, “Look¦18608, your¦18609 father-in-law¦18609 is¦18610 going¦18610 to Timnah¦18611 to shear¦18612 his¦18613 sheep¦18613.”
\v 14 \add So\add* she \add changed out of\add* her¦18617 widow's¦18617 clothes¦18616 and covered¦18619 herself with¦18620 a veil¦18620 and wrapped¦18621 herself \add up\add*. Then¦18615 she sat¦18622 at¦18620 the entrance¦18623 of Enaim¦18624 which¦18625 is¦18626 on¦18620 the road¦18628 to Timnah¦18629. \add She was doing this\add* because¦18630 she had seen¦18631 that¦18625 Shelah¦18635 had grown¦18634 up, but¦18615 she hadn't been¦18639 given¦18639 to him¦18640 as a wife.
\p
\v 15 \add In due course,\add* Yehudah¦18644 saw¦18643 her and¦18643 because¦18647 she had¦18648 covered¦18648 her face¦18649, he¦18643 thought she was a prostitute¦18646.
\v 16 \add So\add* he turned¦18651 to¦18652 her¦18652 beside the¦18655 road¦18655 and¦18651 asked, “Please¦18659 let¦18660 me¦18660 \add lie with\add* you¦18661,” because¦18662 he didn't¦18663 know¦18664 that¦18665 she¦18667 was¦18666 his¦18666 daughter-in-law¦18666.
\p “What¦18669 will¦18671 you give¦18671 me \add in exchange\add*?” she \add asked\add*.
\p
\v 17 “I'll¦18681 send¦18681 \add you\add* a young goat¦18684 from¦18685 my flock¦18687,” he¦18679 replied¦18679.
\p “But¦18679 what about¦18682 \add something to keep now as\add* a pledge¦18692 until¦18693 you send it¦18679,” she¦18688 \add bartered\add*.
\p
\v 18 “What¦18697 pledge¦18698 \add should\add* I¦18700 give¦18700 you¦18702?” he¦18709 \add asked\add*.
\p “Your¦18704 signet \add ring\add* and \add its\add* cord¦18705, and your¦18704 staff¦18706 that¦18699 \add you're holding\add*.” \add So\add* he \add handed\add* them¦18709 to¦18711 her and went¦18712 and lay with¦18708 her, and she¦18714 became¦18714 pregnant¦18714 \add as a result\add*.
\v 19 Then¦18717 \add Tamar\add* got up and left, and she¦18719 took off her veil¦18720 and put¦18722 her widow's¦18724 clothes¦18723 \add back\add* on¦18722.
\p
\v 20 Then¦18728 Yehudah¦18727 sent¦18726 his¦18733 friend the Adullamite¦18734 to¦18735 take¦18735 the young goat¦18731 and¦18726 get the pledge¦18736 back from¦18737 the woman¦18738, but¦18726 he¦18740 couldn't find¦18740 her¦18740,
\v 21 \add so\add* he¦18742 asked¦18742 \add some of the local\add* men¦18745, “Where's¦18748 the temple prostitute¦18749 who was¦18751 at¦18751 Enaim¦18751 beside the road¦18754?”
\p \add But\add* they¦18755 answered¦18755, “There's \add never\add* been a temple prostitute \add around here\add*.”
\p
\v 22 \add So Hirah\add* returned¦18762 to¦18763 Yehudah¦18765 and¦18762 \add ≈told him\add*, “I¦18768 couldn't find¦18768 her¦18768. \add What's more\add*, the men¦18770 of¦18770 the place said¦18772 that there hasn't been a temple prostitute¦18777 at¦18776 that place.”
\p
\v 23 “\add Oh well,\add*” said¦18779 Yehudah¦18780. “Let¦18781 her \add keep those\add* things for¦18783 herself so that¦18790 we¦18785 don't¦18792 become¦18785 a laughingstock¦18786. Anyway, I¦18788 \add did my duty by sending the goat\add*, but¦18779 you¦18793 couldn't find¦18793 her.”
\p
\v 24 About¦18797 three¦18797 months¦18799 later, Yehudah¦18801 was¦18800 told¦18800, “Your¦18805 daughter-in-law¦18805 Tamar¦18804 has¦18803 prostituted¦18803 herself, and¦18806 \add not only that\add*, she's¦18808 pregnant¦18808 from doing it¦18810.”
\p “Bring¦18812 her out¦18812 and let her be¦18813 burnt¦18813 \add to death\add*.” Yehudah \add demanded\add*.
\p
\v 25 \add So they got her and\add* brought¦18816 her out¦18816, \add but\add* she sent¦18818 a message to her father-in-law¦18821, saying¦18830, “It¦18827 was¦18816 the¦18835 man¦18823 who these things belong¦18834 to that got me pregnant¦18829.” Then she \add added\add*, “Please¦18833 identify whose signet ring and cords¦18836 and staff¦18837 these are.”
\p
\v 26 \add When\add* Yehudah¦18841 recognised¦18840 \add the items\add*, he admitted, “She's¦18843 more¦18844 \add in the right\add* than¦18844 I am,\x + \xo 38:26: \xt 37:11.\x* because¦18845 I didn't give¦18852 my¦18854 son¦18854 Shelah¦18853 to¦18859 her.” \add But\add* he didn't \add lie with her again\add*.
\p
\v 27 When the¦18863 time¦18863 came for \add Tamar\add* to give¦18864 birth¦18864, wow, \add she was going to have twins\add*.
\v 28 \add While\add* she¦18870 was¦18869 giving¦18870 birth¦18870, one \add of the babies poked\add* out a hand¦18873 and¦18869 the midwife¦18875 tied¦18876 a scarlet¦18880 thread¦18880 \add around its wrist\add*, saying¦18881, “This¦18882 one came¦18883 out first¦18884.”
\v 29 \add However,\add* it¦18886 pulled its hand¦18889 back in, then¦18886 wow, its brother¦18892 \add was delivered first\add*. Then¦18886 she¦18893\f + \fr 38:28 \ft It's not clear from the text who ‘she’ was—either the mother or the midwife. Similarly, the ‘he’ in the next sentence (and in the next verse) is presumably the father.\f* said¦18893, “How¦18894 \add did you manage to\add* break out first?” So¦18886 he¦18899 named him ‘Perez¦18901’ \add (which means ‘break out’)\add*.
\v 30 After¦18903 that, his brother¦18905 who had the¦18910 scarlet¦18910 thread¦18910 on¦18907 his \add wrist\add* was¦18907 delivered, and¦18903 he¦18911 named him Zerah¦18913.
\c 39
\s1 Yosef and Potifar's wife
\rem /s1 Joseph and Potiphar's Wife; Joseph in Potiphar's House
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* Yosef¦18916 had been¦18917 taken down to Egypt¦18918, and¦18919 an¦18926 Egyptian¦18926 man¦18925 named Potifar had bought¦18919 him from¦18926 the Ishmaelites¦18928 who¦18929 had taken him down there¦18931. \add Potifar was\add* the chief of¦18921 the guards¦18924 for Far'oh¦18922 \add !(Pharaoh¦18922)\add*.
\v 2 Yosef¦18937 \add lived\add* in¦18942 the¦18944 house¦18942 of¦18939 his Egyptian¦18944 master¦18943, and Yahweh¦18934 was with¦18935 him and he¦18938 was successful¦18940 \add at his work\add*.\x + \xo 39:2: \xt Acts 7:9.\x*
\v 3 His¦18947 master¦18947 \add noticed\add* that Yahweh was¦18955 with¦18950 him¦18950 and¦18946 that Yahweh made¦18957 everything¦18951 that he¦18954 did¦18955 succeed¦18957,
\v 4 \add so\add* Yosef¦18961 earned his favour¦18962 as he served¦18964 him. Then¦18960 \add Potifar\add* put him in¦18963 charge of¦18969 his \add entire\add* household¦18969 and¦18960 \add everything that he owned\add*.
\v 5 \add From that time onwards,\add* Yahweh blessed¦18990 that Egyptian¦18995 household¦18994 because¦18996 of¦18994 Yosef¦18997, and¦18978 Yahweh's¦18991 blessing¦18999 \add even extended\add* from¦18995 everything in¦18982 the home \add all the way out\add* to¦19006 \add the\add* countryside¦19008.
\v 6 \add Potifar\add* left¦19010 all¦19011 his belonging in¦19016 Yosef's¦19018 charge¦19016, \add so much so that\add* he didn't¦19019 have to think about¦19011 anything¦19023 else beyond \add what he wanted to eat\add*.
\p \add Now\add* Yosef \add had developed\add* into a well-built and¦19010 handsome¦19034 \add young man\add*,
\v 7 and after¦19041 a while, his master's¦19047 wife¦19045 \add started to develop a crush on\add* him and told \add him\add*, “Come¦19053 to bed with¦19056 me¦19056.”
\v 8 \add But\add* he refused¦19058 and told \add her\add*, “Listen¦19065, with¦19073 me here¦19065, my¦19066 master¦19064 doesn't \add have to\add* think about¦19063 what's¦19071 in the¦19063 house¦19073 and \add he's put me in charge of everything\add*.
\v 9 In¦19085 this house¦19085, he doesn't \add even\add* have¦19090 more¦19087 authority than¦19087 I do and he hasn't kept anything¦19092 back from¦19091 me¦19091, except for¦19093 you¦19096, his¦19100 wife¦19100. How¦19101 could I do this very wicked¦19103 thing and sin¦19106 against¦19107 God¦19107.
\v 10 \add But\add* she kept persisting with Yosef¦19113 day¦19114 \add after\add*, \add but\add* he¦19119 wouldn't lie¦19121 with her¦19122 like she wanted.
\p
\v 11 Then¦19126 \add one\add* day¦19127 when he¦19126 went¦19129 into the house to¦19130 do¦19131 his work and¦19126 there¦19137 were no other men¦19135 of¦19136 the house \add around\add*,
\v 12 \add Potifar's wife\add* grabbed Yosef's cloak and demanded, “\add Come to bed\add* with¦19141 me¦19144.” \add But\add* he¦19145 \add pulled away and\add* fled¦19148 outside¦19150 \add the house\add*, leaving¦19145 her¦19147 \add there\add* holding his¦19141 cloak.
