\p
\v 1 \add Some two years later\add*, \add King\add* Far'oh¦19729 had a dream¦19730 and¦19729 \add in it\add*, he¦19732 was¦19732 standing¦19732 beside the¦19735 Nile \add River\add*.
\v 2 Then¦19740, wow, seven¦19742 fine-looking and well-fed cows¦19743 came¦19741 up¦19741 out of¦19744 the river, and they¦19748 grazed¦19748 among¦19749 the reeds¦19749.
\v 3 \add But\add* wow, seven¦19752 ugly, skinny cows came¦19755 up¦19755 out of¦19760 the river after¦19756 them¦19756, and¦19751 they¦19764 stood¦19764 beside¦19765 the \add other\add* cows on¦19767 the river bank¦19769.
\v 4 Then¦19778 the ugly, skinny cows ate¦19772 the seven¦19779 nice, fat¦19783 cows, and¦19772 then¦19772 Far'oh¦19785 woke up.
\v 5 Then¦19787 he¦19788 fell¦19787 asleep¦19787 \add again\add* and¦19787 had a second¦19789 dream¦19788. And¦19790 wow, seven¦19792 good¦19798, fat¦19797 heads of¦19793 grain¦19793 came¦19794 up¦19794 on¦19795 one¦19796 stalk¦19795.
\v 6 \add But\add* wow, seven¦19801 thin¦19803 heads of grain sprouted after¦19807 them—these heads of grain had been scorched¦19804 by the \add hot, desert\add* wind¦19805.
\v 7 Then¦19809 the thin¦19811 heads of grain¦19814 swallowed¦19809 up the seven¦19813 good, fat¦19815 heads. \add At that point\add*, Far'oh¦19818 woke up, and¦19809 \add realised that\add* it had been a dream¦19820.
\v 8 \add That\add* morning¦19823, Far'oh's¦19840 spirit¦19825 was¦19822 troubled, so he sent¦19826 for all¦19830 the sorcerers and wise¦19838 men of¦19836 Egypt¦19833 and \add he\add* told them his¦19844 dreams¦19844, but¦19822 no one¦19847 could interpret¦19847 them for \add the king\add*.\x + \xo 41:8: \xt Dan 2:2.\x*
\p
\v 9 Then¦19851 the chief¦19852 of¦19852 the cupbearers¦19853 spoke¦19851 with¦19854 Far'oh¦19856 saying¦19857, “I've¦19861 \add just\add* remembered¦19862 my¦19860 \add broken promise\add* today.
\v 10 \add The king\add* had been angry¦19866 with¦19872 his servants¦19869, and he¦19870 put me in the custody¦19872 of the chief of the guards¦19875 \add in his prison\add*—me and the chief of the bakers¦19879.
\v 11 \add The two of us each\add* had a dream¦19882 \add one\add* night¦19883, and¦19881 each of \add the dreams had different interpretations\add*.
\v 12 There¦19892 was a young¦19894 Hebrew¦19895 man¦19894 \add in there\add* with¦19893 us—a slave¦19896 of the chief of the guards¦19898. \add When\add* we¦19899 told him¦19901 our dreams, he¦19903 interpreted¦19903 our \add two different\add* dreams for¦19905 us.
\v 13 And¦19913 it¦19913 \add all\add* happened just¦19914 as¦19914 he'd interpreted¦19915 \add them\add*—I was restored¦19921 to¦19922 my¦19924 position and¦19913 \add the baker\add* was \add executed\add*.
\p
\v 14 \add So\add* Far'oh¦19929 sent¦19928 for Yosef¦19933, and they quickly¦19934 brought him¦19934 \add in\add* from¦19935 the pit¦19937. \add When\add* he'd shaved¦19938 and changed¦19939 his¦19940 clothes¦19940, he \add was brought in\add* to¦19942 \add the king\add*
\v 15 \add who told him\add*, “I \add had\add* a dream but¦19946 no one¦19953 could interpret¦19953 it. \add However,\add* I heard¦19957 that you¦19958 could listen¦19960 to¦19959 a dream and¦19946 \add explain what it means\add*.”
\p
\v 16 “\add No, it's not my ability\add*,” Yosef¦19966 responded, “\add but\add* \em God¦19972\em* will¦19973 answer¦19973 \add the king to give him peace\add*.”
\p
\v 17 “In¦19984 my¦19984 dream¦19984,” Far'oh¦19980 told Yosef¦19983, “wow¦19985, I¦19985 was¦19986 standing¦19986 on¦19984 the¦19989 river bank¦19989
\v 18 and then¦19992 listen, up¦19996 from¦19993 the river came¦19996 seven¦19997 cows¦19998. \add They were\add* well-fed and good-looking, and they¦20003 grazed¦20003 among¦20004 the reeds¦20004.
\v 19 Then¦20006 listen, seven¦20007 other cows came¦20011 up¦20011 after¦20012 them¦20012. \add They were in\add* poor \add condition\add* and very¦20016 ugly and skinny—I¦20021 haven't seen¦20021 cows that ugly \add anywhere in\add* Egypt¦20026.
\v 20 Then¦20033 the skinny, ugly cows ate¦20029 the first¦20036 seven¦20034 fat¦20037 cows,
\v 21 \add but\add* you wouldn't know¦20044 it¦20044 because¦20045 their¦20042 bellies \add were just as skinny as before\add* and¦20039 they looked just as ugly as they had¦20047 before. Then¦20039 I¦20055 woke up.
\p
\v 22 “Then¦20057 I \add had another\add* dream¦20058, and listen, seven¦20061 heads of¦20062 grain¦20062 came¦20063 up¦20063 on¦20058 one¦20065 stalk¦20064. \add They were\add* full¦20066 and good¦20067.
\v 23 Then¦20069 listen, seven¦20070 \add more\add* heads of¦20071 grain¦20071 sprouted after¦20077 them¦20077, \add but they were\add* withered¦20072 and thin¦20073, and scorched¦20074 by the east¦20075 wind¦20075.
\v 24 Then¦20079 the thin¦20082 heads of grain swallowed¦20079 up the seven¦20084 good¦20086 heads of grain. I¦20083 told¦20092 \add these dreams\add* to the sorcerers, but¦20079 no one¦20092 could explain \add them\add* to me¦20093.”
\p
\v 25 Then¦20095 Yosef¦20096 said¦20095 to Far'oh¦20099, “\add The king's\add* dreams¦20100 are one¦20102 \add and the same\add*. God¦20106 has¦20108 shown Far'oh what he's¦20108 going to do¦20107.
\v 26 The¦20113 seven good cows¦20112 are seven years, and¦20117 the seven good heads of grain¦20118 are seven years—\add both\add* dreams¦20123 \add mean the same\add*.
\v 27 And¦20127 the seven thin and ugly cows¦20128 that¦20131 came¦20131 up¦20131 after¦20132 them¦20132 are seven years, and so are the seven thin heads of grain¦20137 scorched¦20139 by the east¦20140 wind¦20140. There will¦20141 be¦20141 seven years of \add drought\add*.
\v 28 That's¦20153 \add why\add* I¦20149 told Far'oh¦20152 that¦20148 God¦20154 has¦20156 shown¦20156 \add him\add* what he's¦20156 going to¦20150 do¦20155.
\v 29 Listen, there's¦20161 seven¦20162 years¦20163 coming¦20164 of plentiful¦20165 \add harvests\add* throughout all¦20167 of Egypt¦20170,
\v 30 \add but\add* seven¦20173 years of \add drought\add* will \add follow\add* after¦20177 them¦20177, \add such that the years of plenty will be quickly\add* forgotten¦20178 and¦20172 famine¦20185 will \add devastate\add* the land¦20188.
\v 31 \add Yes,\add* the \add time of plenty\add* won't¦20190 be remembered because¦20201 of the famine¦20196 \add that follows\add*—it'll¦20192 be very¦20205 \add severe\add*.
\v 32 And¦20207 as for \add having two dreams with the same meaning\add*, it's¦20222 because¦20214 the matter¦20217 has been established by God, and¦20220 God will do¦20222 it soon.
\p
\v 33 “So¦20224 now¦20224 \add the king\add* should¦20225 find a man¦20227 who's discerning¦20228 and wise¦20229, and appoint him¦20225 over¦20231 \add all\add* Egypt¦20234.
\v 34 Then \add the king\add* should¦20238 get him¦20236 to appoint¦20238 supervisors \add all across the country\add* to collect twenty¦20243 percent of the \add crops\add* during the seven¦20248 years¦20249 of plenty¦20250.
\v 35 They¦20271 should collect that¦20260 \add surplus\add* food¦20257 in the coming¦20260 good¦20259 years¦20258, and¦20252 store¦20262 it in the cities¦20270 \add under the king's authority\add*. It¦20268 should be kept¦20271
\v 36 and¦20273 used as a reserve¦20275 for¦20276 the seven¦20277 years¦20278 of¦20278 famine¦20279 that will affect the country, so that the \add people\add* will survive.
\s1 Yosef becomes a ruler in Egypt
\rem /s1 Joseph's Rise to Power; Joseph Is Made Governor over Egypt; Joseph Made Ruler of Egypt
\p
\v 37 \add This idea sounded\add* good¦20290 to Far'oh¦20293 and¦20294 to all¦20295 his¦20297 servants¦20297,
\v 38 so he¦20299 asked them, “\add Do we have any other\add* man¦20306 like¦20305 this¦20305 who has¦20304 God's¦20309 spirit¦20308 in¦20310 him¦20310?”
\v 39 Then¦20312 he¦20312 told Yosef¦20316, “Since¦20313 God¦20319 has¦20318 shown you all¦20323 this¦20325, there's¦20326 no one as discerning¦20328 and¦20312 wise¦20329 as you.
\v 40 You'll be over¦20334 my household¦20336, and¦20337 all¦20341 my people¦20343 will \add obey your every word\add*. Only¦20344 I¦20346 on¦20337 the¦20345 throne will be greater¦20346 than¦20347 you.”\x + \xo 41:40: \xt Acts 7:10.\x*
\rem /s1 Joseph in Charge of Egypt
\v 41 Then¦20349 \add he added\add*, “See¦20354, I've put you¦20356 over¦20357 all¦20358 the¦20360 \add entire country\add*.”
\v 42 Then¦20363 \add he\add* removed¦20363 his signet¦20367 ring from¦20368 his hand and put it¦20370 on¦20372 Yosef's¦20375 hand, and he had him¦20377 dressed in fine¦20380 linen¦20380 clothes¦20376, and put a gold¦20383 chain¦20382 around his neck¦20386.\x + \xo 41:42: \xt Dan 5:29.\x*
\v 43 He¦20388 had \add Yosef\add* ride¦20388 in his¦20396 #2 chariot¦20390, and¦20388 they¦20395 called out in front¦20396 of him, “Bow down!” So¦20388 \add the king\add* put¦20398 him over¦20400 the entire¦20401 \add country\add* of Egypt¦20404.
\v 44 Far'oh \add also\add* told \add him\add*, “I'm¦20411 Far'oh, and¦20406 no person in¦20424 all¦20424 Egypt¦20427 will¦20416 lift¦20416 their hand¦20420 or foot¦20423 without¦20413 \add your permission\add*.”
\v 45 Then¦20429 Far'oh¦20430 gave¦20436 him the Egyptian name¦20431 ‘Zafenath¦20434-Paneah¦20435’ \add (which means ‘the one who reveals secrets’)\add*, and¦20429 he¦20436 \add presented him with\add* Asenath¦20441, the daughter¦20442 of Potifera the priest¦20446 of On¦20447, as a wife. And¦20449 Yosef¦20433 \add travelled throughout\add* the land¦20453 of Egypt¦20454.
\p
\v 46 Yosef¦20456 was¦20457 thirty¦20459 years¦20460 old when¦20461 he'd \add been called to\add* stand¦20461 before¦20462 Far'oh king¦20464 of Egypt. \add When he left\add* Far'oh's¦20463 presence¦20469, he travelled¦20471 throughout the¦20475 land¦20475 of Egypt
\v 47 and¦20478 the land¦20479 produced¦20478 outstanding¦20483 harvests¦20483 during the seven¦20480 years¦20481 of¦20481 plenty¦20482.
\v 48 For seven¦20492 years¦20493, he gathered¦20485 all¦20488 the food throughout the land¦20496 of¦20497 Egypt¦20497 and¦20485 had it¦20507 stored¦20498 in¦20501 the cities¦20501. The¦20490 food from the countryside¦20503 that surrounded each¦20488 city, he put¦20508 inside \add that city\add*.
\v 49 \add So\add* Yosef¦20512 stored¦20511 up¦20511 so much grain¦20513 \add that it was\add* like¦20514 sand¦20514 \add on the beaches\add*, until¦20518 he¦20521 \add eventually\add* stopped measuring¦20522 it because¦20519 it \add too much to calculate\add*.
\p
\v 50 Before the years¦20534 of the famine¦20535 came¦20533, Yosef¦20528 had two¦20530 sons¦20531 that Asenath¦20540 (the daughter¦20541 of Potifera, the priest¦20545 of On¦20546) gave birth to¦20528.
\v 51 Yosef¦20549 named the¦20552 oldest son ‘Manasseh¦20554’ \add (which means ‘forget’)\add*, because¦20555 he¦20557 said, “God¦20558 has¦20557 made¦20557 me¦20557 forget all my hardships¦20563 and¦20548 all of¦20565 my family.”
\v 52 His second¦20572 son he named ‘Efrayim¦20574’ \add (which means ‘fruitful’)\add*, because¦20575 he said, “God¦20578 has¦20577 made¦20577 me¦20577 fruitful in¦20579 the¦20571 land¦20579 of¦20580 my difficulties.”
\p
\v 53 Then¦20582 the¦20584 seven¦20583 years¦20584 of¦20584 plenty¦20585 in¦20588 Egypt¦20589 came¦20582 to¦20582 an¦20582 end¦20582
\v 54 and¦20591 the seven¦20592 years¦20593 of¦20593 famine started¦20591, just¦20596 as¦20596 Yosef¦20598 had¦20597 said¦20597. The¦20594 famine \add covered the entire wider region\add*, but¦20591 \add it was different in\add* Egypt¦20607.\x + \xo 41:54: \xt Acts 7:11.\x*
\v 55 All¦20612 the land¦20614 of¦20614 Egypt¦20615 became famished¦20611, and the people¦20617 cried¦20616 to \add the king\add* for¦20621 food¦20621, so Far'oh¦20620 said¦20622 to all¦20624 the Egyptians, “Go¦20627 to Yosef¦20630 \add and\add* do¦20635 whatever he¦20633 tells¦20633 you.”\x + \xo 41:55: \xt Yhn 2:5.\x*
\v 56 The¦20637 famine was \add right across the country\add*, so Yosef¦20645 opened¦20644 all¦20648 the grain stores and¦20637 sold grain to¦20653 the Egyptians. \add But\add* the famine in¦20656 Egypt¦20657 was \add really\add* severe¦20654.
