\p \add After Yosef had provided for his brothers\add*,
\v 26 they¦21072 loaded¦21072 their grain¦21075 onto their donkeys¦21078 and¦21072 departed¦21079.
\v 27 At¦21095 a lodging¦21090 place¦21090 \add that night\add*, one¦21083 \add of them\add* opened¦21082 his sack to feed his donkey¦21089 and¦21082 \add to his horror\add*, he¦21091 saw¦21091 his silver¦21094 inside the top of the sack.
\v 28 “My¦21108 silver's¦21106 been¦21105 returned¦21105,” he¦21121 told his¦21104 brothers¦21104, “and¦21101 it's¦21105 \add right here\add* in¦21109 my¦21109 sack¦21109!” Then¦21101 their¦21111 \add ≈stomachs tightened\add*, and trembling¦21112, they¦21112 \add ≈asked\add* each¦21113 other, “What's¦21118 this¦21120 that¦21120 God's¦21122 doing¦21121 to¦21123 us¦21123?”
\p
\v 29 \add When\add* they \add got home\add* to Yacob¦21128 their¦21129 father¦21129 in Canaan¦21131, they told¦21132 him¦21133 everything that¦21137 had¦21137 happened¦21137 to them¦21138,
\v 30 “\add This\add* man¦21142, the¦21142 master¦21143 of¦21143 \add Egypt\add*, spoke¦21141 harshly¦21146 to us and¦21147 treated us as¦21149 if we were spying¦21149 out¦21149 \add his country\add*,
\v 31 but¦21154 we told him¦21155, ‘We're honest¦21156—we're¦21159 not¦21158 spies¦21160.
\v 32 We're twelve brothers¦21166, the sons¦21167 of¦21167 our father. One¦21169 is \add no longer\add* alive, and the youngest¦21171 is with¦21173 our father today \add back\add* in¦21176 Canaan¦21177.’
\v 33 Then¦21179 \add that\add* man¦21181 told us¦21180, ‘\add I'll find out if you're honest\add*: Leave¦21191 one¦21190 of¦21195 your brothers¦21189 with¦21184 me¦21192, and take¦21197 grain for your families and go¦21198.
\v 34 Then bring¦21200 me¦21205 your youngest brother so¦21206 that I'll know¦21206 you're not¦21208 spies¦21209—that you're \add telling the truth\add*. Then I'll \add release\add* your brother and¦21200 you can trade \add here freely\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 35 Then¦21224 \add when\add* they \add started\add* emptying¦21226 their sacks¦21227, wow, each¦21230 man's¦21230 pouch of silver was¦21234 inside his¦21234 sack¦21234. \add When the brothers\add* and¦21224 their father¦21241 saw¦21235 their pouches of silver, they were¦21242 afraid¦21242.
\v 36 “You've \add deprived\add* me \add of another son\add*,” Yacob¦21246 \add exclaimed\add*. Yosef¦21250 is¦21251 \add gone\add*! Simeon's¦21252 \add gone\add*! And¦21252 \add then you want to take\add* Benyamin¦21256 \add as well\add*! \add Why's everything going so wrong for\add* me?”
\v 37 Then¦21262 Reuben¦21263 \add offered\add* to his¦21266 father¦21266, “You¦21272 can kill¦21272 my¦21282 two¦21270 sons¦21271 if¦21273 I don't¦21275 bring¦21276 \add Benyamin back\add* to you. \add Let me take responsibility for him\add*, and¦21262 I'll¦21276 \add make sure that he\add* returns¦21284 to you.”
\v 38 “My¦21291 son¦21291 will¦21290 \em not\em* go¦21290 with¦21302 you,” Yacob¦21312 countered, “because¦21293 his¦21295 brother¦21295 is dead, and¦21287 he¦21287 alone¦21298 is left. And¦21300 if¦21293 harm¦21301 were to meet him¦21300 on the road that¦21303 you're going on, then¦21287 you'd bring¦21307 my¦21310 grey¦21310 head down¦21290 to the grave in¦21302 grief.”
\c 43
\s1 Yosef's brothers bring Benyamin to Egypt
\rem /s1 Joseph's Brothers Return to Egypt with Benjamin; The Brothers Come Again, Bringing Benjamin; The Brothers Return to Egypt; The Second Journey to Egypt
\p
\v 1 \add However,\add* the famine¦21314 in the region was¦21315 \add still\add* very severe,
\v 2 \add so\add* when they'd \add used up all\add* the¦21324 grain¦21324 that¦21325 they'd brought¦21326 from¦21327 Egypt¦21327, their¦21330 father¦21330 \add instructed\add* them¦21329, “Go¦21322 back¦21331 \add there and\add* buy¦21332 \add some\add* food¦21337 for¦21334 us¦21334.”
\p
\v 3 \add But\add* Yehudah¦21341 \add challenged\add* him¦21340, “\add That\add* man¦21346 \add ≈told us that he¦21344 won't¦21348 even see¦21350 us\add* unless \add our\add* brother¦21353 is¦21354 with¦21345 \add us\add*.
\v 4 We¦21360 can \add only\add* go¦21364 \add there\add* to buy¦21365 food¦21367 for¦21366 you if you'll \add agree to\add* send¦21359 our¦21362 \add youngest\add* brother¦21362 with¦21363 us¦21363.
\v 5 If¦21375 you¦21371 don't¦21371, \add it's not even worth going, because that\add* man¦21377 \add clearly told us that he won't even see us\add* unless \add our\add* brother¦21385 is¦21386 with¦21386 \add us\add*.
\p
\v 6 “Why¦21390 did¦21391 you all treat me¦21392 badly by telling the man¦21394 you have \add a younger\add* brother¦21397?” \add their father\add* Yisra'el¦21389 asked.
\p
\v 7 “The¦21403 man¦21403 \add kept asking\add* about¦21405 us¦21404 and¦21399 our relatives,” they¦21399 answered. “\add He asked\add*, ‘Is¦21408 your father¦21408 still¦21407 alive? Do you have another brother?’ \add We just answered what he asked\add*. How could we possibly¦21422 know¦21423 that he would \add insist that we bring our youngest brother there\add*?”
\p
\v 8 Then¦21431 Yehudah¦21432 said¦21431 to his¦21436 father¦21436, “\add Entrust\add* the¦21438 young¦21438 man¦21438 to me¦21439, and we'll¦21440 \add pack\add* up and go¦21441 so¦21442 that¦21442 both¦21445 we and you¦21450 and our¦21453 little¦21453 ones will¦21441 live¦21442 and not¦21443 die¦21444.
\v 9 I myself will¦21456 guarantee him. \add If anything happens to him, you can charge it to me\add*. If I don't¦21461 \add get\add* him \add safely\add* back to you, then I'll¦21456 \add personally\add* carry the blame¦21466 \add for the rest of my life\add*.
\v 10 \add If we hadn't sat around here doing nothing for so long\add*, certainly by now¦21477 we could have¦21478 \add been there and back\add* twice!”
\p
\v 11 “\add Well, if that's our only choice\add*,” their¦21485 father¦21485 \add acceded\add*, “then¦21482 do¦21492 this¦21491: Take¦21493 some of¦21496 the best¦21494 produce¦21494 \add from around here\add* in¦21496 your bags, and take¦21497 it¦21482 down¦21497 to¦21498 the man¦21498 as a gift¦21499: a little balm¦21501, a little honey¦21503, spices¦21504 and myrrh¦21505, \add and some\add* pistachio¦21506 nuts¦21506 and almonds¦21507.
\v 12 And¦21509 take¦21511 double¦21510 the \add amount of\add* silver, because you must¦21519 repay the silver that¦21516 was returned¦21516 in¦21512 the \add tops\add* of your¦21512 sacks¦21518. Perhaps¦21521 it¦21523 was a mistake¦21522.
\v 13 So¦21525 take¦21528 your¦21527 brother¦21527 and \add go and\add* return to¦21531 the¦21533 man¦21533.
\v 14 May¦21537 the provider God¦21535 Almighty¦21536 \add cause\add* the man¦21541 to treat you kindly, and may¦21537 he¦21542 send your other¦21547 brother¦21546 and Benyamin¦21550 \add back\add* with you. And¦21551 \add as for me\add*, if I \add lose another son\add*, I'll \add just have to mourn him\add*.”
\p
\v 15 \add So\add* the \add brothers\add* took¦21556 the gifts¦21560 and double¦21562 the \add amount of\add* silver. Then¦21556 \add taking\add* Benyamin¦21569, they¦21570 \add travelled\add* down¦21571 to Egypt¦21572 and \add presented themselves\add* to Yosef¦21575.
\v 16 \add When\add* Yosef¦21578 saw¦21577 Benyamin¦21582 with them¦21579, he¦21583 told \add his head house-servant\add*, “Take \add these\add* men¦21591 to¦21592 the¦21592 house, and slaughter¦21593 an animal and prepare¦21595 a meal, because¦21596 \add they'll\add* eat¦21598 with me¦21597 at¦21600 noon.”
\v 17 The¦21613 man¦21603 did what \add he'd\add* been instructed and¦21602 brought¦21607 the¦21608 men¦21611 to¦21612 Yosef's¦21606 house¦21612.
\p
\v 18 \add But\add* the \add brothers\add* were¦21618 afraid¦21615 when they \add found out that they'd been\add* brought to \add wait outside\add* Yosef's¦21620 house¦21619, and said¦21621 \add to each other\add*, “We¦21629 must have been brought \add here\add* because¦21617 of¦21624 the silver¦21625 that was returned¦21626 in¦21627 our sacks¦21627 the first¦21628 time¦21628. \add He wants to overpower us, then arrest\add* us and \add confiscate\add* our donkeys¦21640 and take¦21635 us as slaves¦21637.”
